OAI_PAPER({"slug": "two-limit-cycles-for-quintic-lienard-systems-September-24-2026", "title": "Two limit cycles for quintic Liénard systems", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/two-limit-cycles-for-quintic-lienard-systems-September-24-2026/two-limit-cycles-for-quintic-lienard-systems-September-24-2026.pdf", "family": "143", "family_title": "Hilbert's sixteenth problem: uniform bounds for limit cycles", "abstract_html": "Every classical Liénard system \\(\\dot x=y-F(x)\\), \\(\\dot y=-x\\), with <i>F</i> an arbitrary real polynomial of degree at most five, has at most two geometrically distinct isolated periodic orbits, and the bound is attained. This proves the degree-five case of the Lins Neto–de Melo–Pugh conjecture.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The system has at most two limit cycles in \\(\\mathbb R^2\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(F\\in\\mathbb R[x]\\) satisfy \\(\\deg F\\leq 5\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Consider the classical Liénard system\n  \\[\n  \\dot x=y-F(x),\\qquad \\dot y=-x.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A limit cycle is the image of a nonconstant periodic solution that is isolated among periodic orbits. Each image is counted once, irrespective of multiplicity, stability, or hyperbolicity. Periodic orbits belonging to a center are not limit cycles.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The system has at most two limit cycles in \\(\\mathbb R^2\\). Some systems in this class have two limit cycles. Thus the exact maximum is two.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 12 proves that every classical Liénard system with \\(\\deg F\\leq5\\) has at most two geometrically distinct isolated periodic orbits. Proposition 13 supplies degree-five members with two such orbits. Therefore the exact maximum is two, as asserted.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "Lemma 2.1 and the transport identities of Section 2.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every \\(0<h<t\\), the two intersections exist, are unique and transverse, and satisfy \\( y_<(h,t)<\\phi(h)<y_>(h,t). \\) The intervening arc has a unique maximum \\(u=t\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\phi\\in C^\\infty((0,\\infty))\\), and consider\n  \\[\n  \\frac{du}{dy}=\\phi(u)-y.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(t&gt;0\\), let \\(u(y;t)\\) be the solution through\n  \\[\n  u(\\phi(t);t)=t.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(0&lt;h&lt;t\\), denote the two intersections with \\(u=h\\) by \\(y_&lt;(h,t)\\) and \\(y_&gt;(h,t)\\), and set\n  \\[\n  r(h,t)=\\frac{y_&gt;(h,t)-y_&lt;(h,t)}2,\\qquad\n  M(h,t)=\\frac{y_&gt;(h,t)+y_&lt;(h,t)}2-\\phi(h).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">After replacing \\(t\\) by the width coordinate \\(r\\), write\n  \\[\n  v=M_r,\\quad k(h)=\\phi'(h),\\quad q=r^2-M^2,\\quad\n  \\alpha=\\frac rq,\\quad D=\\partial_h-\\alpha\\partial_r.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(0&lt;h&lt;t\\), the two intersections exist, are unique and transverse, and satisfy\n\\[\ny_&lt;(h,t)&lt;\\phi(h)&lt;y_&gt;(h,t).\n\\]\nThe intervening arc has a unique maximum \\(u=t\\). At fixed \\(h\\),\n\\[\n(y_&lt;)_t&lt;0&lt;(y_&gt;)_t,\n\\]\nboth derivatives are nonzero, and \\(t\\mapsto r(h,t)\\) is a smooth increasing bijection from \\((h,\\infty)\\) onto \\((0,\\infty)\\). Consequently \\(M\\) is smooth on \\((0,\\infty)^2\\), and\n\\[\n|M|&lt;r,\\qquad |v|&lt;1,\\qquad r^2\\geq 2(t-h).\n\\]\nAt fixed width, the base height is strictly increasing with peak height:\n\\[\n\\left.\\frac{\\partial h}{\\partial t}\\right|_r&gt;0.\n\\]</p><p>Along a fixed-peak arc,\n\\[\n\\left.\\frac{dr}{dh}\\right|_t=-\\frac r{r^2-M^2},\\qquad\n\\left.\\frac{dM}{dh}\\right|_t=\\frac M{r^2-M^2}-k(h),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nDM=\\frac Mq-k(h),\\qquad\nDv=-\\frac{2Mr(1-v^2)}{q^2}.\n\\]\nFor\n\\[\n\\theta=\\operatorname{arctanh}(M/r),\\qquad\nL=\\operatorname{arctanh}v,\n\\]\na prime denoting total differentiation along a fixed-peak characteristic parametrized by increasing width, one has\n\\[\nh'=-r\\operatorname{sech}^2\\theta,\\qquad\n\\theta'=k(h)-\\frac{\\sinh(2\\theta)}r,\\qquad\nL'=\\frac{\\sinh(2\\theta)}r,\n\\]\n\\[\nk(h)'=-r\\operatorname{sech}^2\\theta\\,\\phi''(h),\\qquad\nt-\\frac{r^2}{2}\\leq h\\leq t.\n\\]</p><p>If \\(\\phi\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb R)\\), the same conclusions hold for arbitrary real \\(h&lt;t\\). At each fixed peak \\(t\\), decreasing \\(h\\) from \\(t\\) to \\(-\\infty\\) parametrizes the full range \\(0&lt;r&lt;\\infty\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 1.1 and 1.2 establish the global coordinates and the endpoint transport formulas.</p><p>For any smooth \\(f(h,r)\\),\n\\[\nD(f_r)=(Df)_r+\\alpha_rf_r,\\qquad\n\\alpha_r=\\frac1q-\\frac{2r(r-Mv)}{q^2}.\n\\]\nUsing \\(DM=M/q-k(h)\\), one obtains\n\\[\nDv=\\frac{2v}{q}\n-\\frac{2(M+rv)(r-Mv)}{q^2}\n=-\\frac{2Mr(1-v^2)}{q^2}.\n\\]</p><p>Along a characteristic parametrized by increasing width,\n\\[\nf'=-\\frac{Df}{\\alpha}.\n\\]\nIn particular,\n\\[\nM'=-\\frac Mr+\\frac qr\\,k(h).\n\\]\nSubstituting \\(M=r\\tanh\\theta\\) and \\(v=\\tanh L\\) gives the stated angle equations and the equation for \\(k(h)'\\). Finally \\(0\\leq t-h\\leq r^2/2\\) gives the displayed height bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "the existence and uniqueness part of Lemma 2.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In the setting of Proposition 1, for every \\(0<h<t\\), the solution through \\((y,u)=(\\phi(t),t)\\) reaches \\(u=h\\) exactly once on each side of its peak.", "statement": "<p>In the setting of Proposition 1, for every \\(0&lt;h&lt;t\\), the solution through \\((y,u)=(\\phi(t),t)\\) reaches \\(u=h\\) exactly once on each side of its peak. These intersections occur at finite \\(y\\), are transverse, depend smoothly on \\((h,t)\\), and satisfy\n\\[\ny_&lt;&lt;\\phi(h)&lt;y_&gt;.\n\\]\nThe intervening arc has a unique maximum \\(u=t\\). The same assertion holds on every compact interval \\([h,t]\\) in the smooth domain of \\(\\phi\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Differentiating the arc equation gives\n\\[\nu_{yy}=\\phi'(u)u_y-1.\n\\]\nThus every critical point in the profile domain is a nondegenerate maximum, with \\(u_{yy}=-1\\). On either side of the prescribed peak the height decreases strictly as one moves away from it, until the level \\(h\\) is reached. A second critical point on either branch would require a minimum between two maxima, which is impossible.</p><p>These intersections occur at finite \\(y\\). Indeed, while a branch remains in \\([h,t]\\), the profile is bounded there. For sufficiently large positive \\(y\\), the arc equation gives \\(u_y&lt;-1\\); for sufficiently large negative \\(y\\), it gives \\(u_y&gt;1\\). In the respective outward directions these inequalities force the branch to reach \\(h\\). There is also no finite-\\(y\\) failure of continuation while \\(u\\) stays in the compact interval \\([h,t]\\). The strict derivative signs at the intersections give the endpoint inequalities.</p><p>All endpoints depend smoothly on \\((h,t)\\), by smooth dependence of the differential equation and transversality.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "the endpoint-variation and global-coordinate part of Lemma 2.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In the setting of Proposition 1, define \\( \\xi(y;t)=\\partial_tu(y;t), \\) with \\(y\\) held fixed.", "statement": "<p>In the setting of Proposition 1, define\n\\[\n\\xi(y;t)=\\partial_tu(y;t),\n\\]\nwith \\(y\\) held fixed. Then \\(\\xi&gt;0\\), and\n\\[\n(y_&lt;)_t=-\\frac{\\xi(y_&lt;;t)}{r-M}&lt;0,\\qquad\n(y_&gt;)_t=\\frac{\\xi(y_&gt;;t)}{r+M}&gt;0.\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\n(y_&lt;)_h=\\frac1{r-M},\\qquad\n(y_&gt;)_h=-\\frac1{r+M}.\n\\]\nThe global width parametrization, the inequalities\n\\[\n|M|&lt;r,\\qquad |M_r|&lt;1,\\qquad r^2\\geq2(t-h),\n\\]\nand the fixed-width inequality\n\\[\n\\left.\\frac{\\partial h}{\\partial t}\\right|_r&gt;0\n\\]\nfollow.</p>", "proof": "<p>Differentiating the moving initial condition gives\n\\[\n\\xi(\\phi(t);t)=1,\n\\]\nbecause \\(u_y=0\\) at the peak. Moreover,\n\\[\n\\xi_y=\\phi'(u)\\xi,\n\\]\nso \\(\\xi&gt;0\\) throughout the arc. Differentiating the endpoint equations gives the displayed peak derivatives. In particular \\(r_t&gt;0\\). After changing from \\(t\\) to \\(r\\),\n\\[\nM_r=\n\\frac{(y_&gt;)_t+(y_&lt;)_t}{(y_&gt;)_t-(y_&lt;)_t}\\in(-1,1).\n\\]\nThe inequality \\(|M|&lt;r\\) follows directly from the endpoint inequalities.</p><p>At fixed \\(t\\), differentiation with respect to \\(h\\) gives the displayed height derivatives, and hence\n\\[\n\\left.\\frac{dr}{dh}\\right|_t=-\\frac r{r^2-M^2},\\qquad\n\\left.\\frac{dM}{dh}\\right|_t=\\frac M{r^2-M^2}-\\phi'(h).\n\\]\nThe first identity implies\n\\[\n\\frac{d(r^2)}{dh}\\leq-2.\n\\]\nThe nondegenerate maximum gives \\(r\\to0\\) as \\(t\\downarrow h\\), or as \\(h\\uparrow t\\) with the peak fixed. Integration yields\n\\[\nr^2\\geq2(t-h).\n\\]\nAt fixed \\(h\\), this implies \\(r\\to\\infty\\) as \\(t\\to\\infty\\). Together with \\(r_t&gt;0\\), it proves the asserted bijection and its smooth inverse. Also,\n\\[\n\\left.\\frac{\\partial h}{\\partial t}\\right|_r=-\\frac{r_t}{r_h}&gt;0.\n\\]</p><p>The same proof applies to any compact interval in the smooth domain. For an entire smooth profile, the bound \\(r^2\\geq2(t-h)\\) proves that \\(r\\to\\infty\\) as \\(h\\to-\\infty\\) at fixed peak.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Lemmas 2.2 and 2.3, Equations (2.9), (2.12), and (2.13)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "At fixed regular base height \\(h\\) and width \\(r>0\\), \\( \\bigl[(M+r)K(y_>)-(M-r)K(y_<)\\bigr]\\delta Y =\\int_{y_<}^{y_>}K(y)\\psi(u(y))\\,dy. \\) Its left coefficient is strictly positive, and …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Arc midpoints and widths are those of Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">A regular base height means a height having a neighborhood on which the profile is smooth.</p><p class=\"li\">For a smooth family \\(\\phi_\\eta\\), with \\(\\phi_0=\\phi\\), write\n  \\[\n  \\psi(u)=\\left.\\partial_\\eta\\phi_\\eta(u)\\right|_{\\eta=0},\\qquad\n  Y=M+\\phi(h),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  K(y)=\\exp\\left(-\\int_{y_&lt;}^{y}\\phi'(u(z))\\,dz\\right).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>At fixed regular base height \\(h\\) and width \\(r&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\bigl[(M+r)K(y_&gt;)-(M-r)K(y_&lt;)\\bigr]\\delta Y\n=\\int_{y_&lt;}^{y_&gt;}K(y)\\psi(u(y))\\,dy.\n\\]\nIts left coefficient is strictly positive, and\n\\[\n\\delta M=\\delta Y-\\psi(h).\n\\]\nConsequently, if\n\\[\n\\phi_1(u)-\\phi_1(h)&gt;\\phi_0(u)-\\phi_0(h)\\qquad(u&gt;h),\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\nM_1(h,r)&gt;M_0(h,r)\\qquad(r&gt;0).\n\\]</p><p>Near any regular base height, with \\(k=\\phi'\\), there are smooth remainder functions such that\n\\[\nM(h,r)=\\frac13k(h)r^2+r^3A(h,r),\n\\]\n\\[\nv(h,r)=\\frac23k(h)r+r^2B(h,r),\n\\]\n\\[\nt(h,r)=h+r^2\\left(\\frac12+rC(h,r)\\right).\n\\]\nThe remainders extend smoothly through \\(r=0\\), jointly with finite-dimensional smooth profile parameters. In particular, the expansions may be differentiated, and\n\\[\n\\left.\\frac{\\partial h}{\\partial t}\\right|_r\\longrightarrow1.\n\\]</p><p>At the zero profile, at base height zero and width \\(w&gt;0\\), the first variations are\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|cc}\n\\psi(s)&amp;\\delta M(0,w)&amp;\\delta M_w(0,w)\\\\ \\hline\ns&amp;w^2/3&amp;2w/3\\\\\ns^2/2&amp;w^4/15&amp;4w^3/15\\\\\ns^3/6&amp;w^6/105&amp;6w^5/105.\n\\end{array}\n\\]</p><p>Along a characteristic with smooth peak \\(t\\),\n\\[\n\\theta(r)=\\frac13k(t)r+O(r^2),\\qquad\nL(r)=\\frac23k(t)r+O(r^2),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nL(r)=\\int_0^r\\frac{\\sinh(2\\theta(s))}{s}\\,ds.\n\\]\nFor a quadratic profile with peak slope \\(z\\), curvature \\(\\kappa\\), and moving base slope \\(j\\), locally uniformly for finite \\((z,\\kappa)\\),\n\\[\n\\theta(s,z,\\kappa)=\\frac13zs+O(s^2),\\qquad\n\\theta_z(s,z,\\kappa)=\\frac13s+O(s^2),\n\\]\n\\[\nL(s,z,\\kappa)=\\frac23zs+O(s^2),\\qquad\nj(s,z,\\kappa)=z+O(s^2),\\qquad j_z\\longrightarrow1.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 2.1 proves the variation and comparison assertions. At the zero profile,\n\\[\nu_w(y)=\\frac{w^2-y^2}{2},\\qquad -w\\leq y\\leq w,\n\\]\nand \\(M=0\\), \\(K=1\\). Thus, when \\(\\psi(0)=0\\),\n\\[\n\\delta M(0,w)=\\frac1{2w}\\int_{-w}^{w}\\psi(u_w(y))\\,dy.\n\\]\nDirect integration gives\n\\[\n\\int_{-w}^{w}u_w\\,dy=\\frac{2w^3}{3},\\qquad\n\\int_{-w}^{w}\\frac{u_w^2}{2}\\,dy=\\frac{2w^5}{15},\\qquad\n\\int_{-w}^{w}\\frac{u_w^3}{6}\\,dy=\\frac{2w^7}{105}.\n\\]\nSmooth dependence permits differentiation with respect to positive width, proving the table.</p><p>Lemma 2.2 proves the smooth small-width expansions. Along a fixed smooth peak, Proposition 1 gives \\(h=t+O(r^2)\\), so the displayed expansions for \\(\\theta\\) and \\(L\\) follow. Both angles tend to zero; integrating\n\\[\nL'=\\frac{\\sinh(2\\theta)}r\n\\]\ngives the asserted integral, whose integrand is bounded near zero.</p><p>For a quadratic profile, translate the peak to height zero and apply the local expansions. Smooth parameter-dependent remainders give the differentiated statements for \\(\\theta_z\\) and \\(j_z\\), rather than differentiation of an unspecified error bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Lemma 2.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For a smooth family of profiles \\(\\phi_\\eta\\), all defined on a neighborhood of the relevant compact arcs, fix a regular base height \\(h\\) and width \\(r>0\\).", "statement": "<p>For a smooth family of profiles \\(\\phi_\\eta\\), all defined on a neighborhood of the relevant compact arcs, fix a regular base height \\(h\\) and width \\(r&gt;0\\). With the notation of Proposition 2, the first variation satisfies\n\\[\n\\bigl[(M+r)K(y_&gt;)-(M-r)K(y_&lt;)\\bigr]\\delta Y\n=\\int_{y_&lt;}^{y_&gt;}K(y)\\psi(u(y))\\,dy.\n\\]\nThe coefficient of \\(\\delta Y\\) is strictly positive, and \\(\\delta M=\\delta Y-\\psi(h)\\). The strict translated-profile comparison in Proposition 2 follows.</p>", "proof": "<p>Both endpoint coordinates have variation \\(\\delta Y\\), since their difference \\(2r\\) is fixed. Let \\(\\delta u\\) denote variation at fixed \\(y\\). Differentiating the differential equation and endpoint conditions gives\n\\[\n(\\delta u)_y=\\phi'(u)\\delta u+\\psi(u),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\delta u(y_&lt;)=(M-r)\\delta Y,\\qquad\n\\delta u(y_&gt;)=(M+r)\\delta Y.\n\\]\nMultiplication by \\(K\\) and integration prove the variation formula. Its coefficient is positive because \\(K&gt;0\\), \\(M+r&gt;0\\), and \\(M-r&lt;0\\).</p><p>For the comparison, translate each endpoint coordinate by its own base value \\(\\phi_i(h)\\). This replaces its profile by \\(\\phi_i(u)-\\phi_i(h)\\), without changing \\(M_i\\). Interpolate linearly between the translated profiles. The variation vanishes at the base and is strictly positive in every arc interior. The variation formula makes the midpoint derivative strictly positive along the interpolation. Integration in the interpolation parameter proves the comparison.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "Lemma 2.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For a profile smooth near a regular base height, the small-width data have the smooth expansions stated in Proposition 2, jointly with any finite-dimensional smooth family of profiles.", "statement": "<p>For a profile smooth near a regular base height, the small-width data have the smooth expansions stated in Proposition 2, jointly with any finite-dimensional smooth family of profiles.</p>", "proof": "<p>For positive \\(r\\), introduce\n\\[\ns=\\frac{u-h}{r^2},\\qquad\n\\zeta=\\frac{y-\\phi(h)}r.\n\\]\nThe scaled equation is\n\\[\n\\frac{ds}{d\\zeta}=g(s)-\\zeta,\\qquad\ng(s)=\\frac{\\phi(h+r^2s)-\\phi(h)}r.\n\\]\nIts relative midpoint at scaled width one is \\(M(h,r)/r\\). If the scaled profile is held fixed while its width \\(w\\) varies near one, its midpoint is \\(M(h,rw)/r\\), so its width derivative at \\(w=1\\) is \\(v(h,r)\\).</p><p>Introduce a signed auxiliary parameter \\(\\rho\\), while keeping the scaled width positive and near one, and define\n\\[\ng(h,\\rho,s)=\\rho s\\int_0^1\\phi'(h+\\tau\\rho^2s)\\,d\\tau.\n\\]\nChoose a fixed compact scaled-height interval containing a neighborhood of \\([0,1/2]\\), and restrict \\(h\\) to a neighborhood of the given regular height. For sufficiently small \\(|\\rho|\\), this is a smooth family through \\(\\rho=0\\), equals the difference quotient for \\(\\rho\\neq0\\), and is the zero profile at \\(\\rho=0\\). Negative \\(\\rho\\) is only a parameter; no negative physical width is used.</p><p>At the zero profile, an arc of scaled peak height \\(T\\) has endpoints \\(\\pm\\sqrt{2T}\\), and its width derivative with respect to \\(T\\) equals one at \\(T=1/2\\). Smooth dependence, endpoint transversality, and the implicit function theorem give smooth peak, midpoint, and midpoint-width-derivative data at scaled width one. Only a neighborhood of the compact parabolic arc is used.</p><p>The derivative of \\(g\\) with respect to \\(\\rho\\) at zero is \\(k(h)s\\). The first variations at the zero profile give derivatives \\(k(h)/3\\) and \\(2k(h)/3\\) for the scaled midpoint and its width derivative. Their values at zero are zero; the scaled peak is \\(1/2+O(\\rho)\\). Taylor’s formula with a smooth remainder, followed by \\(\\rho=r&gt;0\\), proves the expansions and their joint smoothness. Finally \\(t_h=1+O(r^2)\\) at fixed \\(r\\), so the inverse derivative tends to one.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Proposition 2.5, with the boundary data of Lemma 2.4", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The limiting endpoints are finite and satisfy \\(a(t)\\leq0\\leq b(t)\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(F\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb R)\\), with \\(F(0)=0\\), and consider\n  \\[\n  \\dot x=y-F(x),\\qquad \\dot y=-x.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  \\phi_\\pm(u)=F(\\pm\\sqrt{2u}),\\qquad u\\geq0.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For a profile \\(\\phi\\in C^\\infty((0,\\infty))\\cap C([0,\\infty))\\), with \\(\\phi(0)=0\\), use the arc coordinates of Proposition 1 and define\n  \\[\n  a(t)=\\lim_{h\\downarrow0}y_&lt;(h,t),\\qquad\n  b(t)=\\lim_{h\\downarrow0}y_&gt;(h,t),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  T_\\phi=\\{t&gt;0:a(t)&lt;0&lt;b(t)\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(I_\\phi\\) be the width image of \\(T_\\phi\\), and set \\(I_\\phi=\\varnothing\\) if \\(T_\\phi=\\varnothing\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(M_\\pm(0,r)\\) be the boundary midpoints for \\(\\phi_\\pm\\), and put\n  \\[\n  I=I_+\\cap I_-,\\qquad\n  \\Delta(r)=M_+(0,r)-M_-(0,r).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The limiting endpoints are finite and satisfy \\(a(t)\\leq0\\leq b(t)\\). The set \\(T_\\phi\\) is empty or an open ray. On it,\n\\[\na_t&lt;0&lt;b_t,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nr(0,t)=\\frac{b(t)-a(t)}2\n\\]\nis a smooth increasing bijection onto an open width ray \\(I_\\phi\\). On \\(I_\\phi\\), \\(M(0,r)\\) is smooth,\n\\[\n|M(0,r)|&lt;r,\\qquad |M_r(0,r)|&lt;1,\n\\]\nand all partial \\(r\\)-derivatives of \\(M(h,r)\\) converge locally uniformly to their boundary values as \\(h\\downarrow0\\).</p><p>The images of nonconstant periodic solutions are in one-to-one correspondence with the zeros of \\(\\Delta\\) on the single open interval \\(I\\). Each periodic orbit has exactly one positive and one negative intersection with the \\(y\\)-axis. Isolated periodic orbits correspond exactly to isolated zeros of \\(\\Delta\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 3.1 supplies the boundary data. On either open half-plane, \\(y\\) is strictly monotone in physical time, and \\(u=x^2/2\\) gives\n\\[\n\\frac{du}{dy}=F(x)-y=\\phi_\\pm(u)-y.\n\\]\nThus every half-plane excursion is an arc.</p><p>Every nonconstant periodic solution has both signs of \\(x\\), since integration of \\(\\dot y=-x\\) over a period gives \\(\\int x\\,dt=0\\). The only equilibrium is \\((0,0)\\), which a nonconstant orbit cannot contain. At an axis intersection, \\(\\dot x=y\\neq0\\), so the intersection is transverse. There are finitely many intersections in a period: an accumulation would contradict transversality or would reach the equilibrium.</p><p>A positive-axis intersection enters \\(x&gt;0\\), and a negative-axis intersection enters \\(x&lt;0\\). Between consecutive intersections, the transformed height is positive, vanishes at its endpoints, and has a unique positive peak. The endpoint signs are strict, so the arc belongs to the transverse boundary domain.</p><p>For either profile, increasing the positive endpoint strictly decreases the negative endpoint. Hence the right-half-plane endpoint map and the left-half-plane endpoint map are both strictly decreasing. Their composition on positive intersections is strictly increasing. An increasing map cannot have a finite periodic sequence of distinct points: if one iterate is larger or smaller than its predecessor, strict order persists. Thus a closed orbit has one distinct positive and one distinct negative intersection and consists of one arc of each sign.</p><p>The two arcs of a periodic orbit have equal endpoints, hence equal widths and midpoints, giving a zero of \\(\\Delta\\). Conversely, a zero at width \\(r\\) gives common endpoints\n\\[\na=M_\\pm(0,r)-r&lt;0,\\qquad b=M_\\pm(0,r)+r&gt;0.\n\\]\nRecover\n\\[\nx=\\sqrt{2u_+(y)}\\quad\\text{on the right},\\qquad\nx=-\\sqrt{2u_-(y)}\\quad\\text{on the left},\n\\]\nand introduce time by \\(\\dot y=-x\\). On the open arcs,\n\\[\n\\dot x=-u_y=y-F(x).\n\\]\nAt their endpoints,\n\\[\n(u_\\pm)_y(a)=-a&gt;0,\\qquad (u_\\pm)_y(b)=-b&lt;0.\n\\]\nThe zeros are simple, so \\(dy/\\sqrt{2u_\\pm(y)}\\) is integrable there. Travel times are finite, and the reconstructed derivatives extend with values \\((\\dot x,\\dot y)=(y,0)\\). The arcs join as a solution of the original smooth vector field; uniqueness gives a closed orbit.</p><p>Different roots give different positive intersections, because\n\\[\n\\frac d{dr}\\bigl(r+M_+(0,r)\\bigr)=1+M_{+,r}(0,r)&gt;0.\n\\]\nThis proves the bijection.</p><p>Fix a matched orbit with positive intersection \\((0,s_0)\\) and first positive return time \\(T\\). On a short positive-axis section \\(S\\), the first return ordinate \\(P(s)\\) and time \\(\\tau(s)\\) are smooth, with \\(\\tau(s)\\to T\\). Disjoint small time windows around the reference crossings, together with \\(|x|\\) bounded away from zero on the intervening compact time segments, show that nearby solutions have exactly the nearby crossings and no others. The coordinate\n\\[\ns=r+M_+(0,r)\n\\]\nis locally smooth and strictly increasing, and\n\\[\n\\Delta(r)=0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad P(s)=s.\n\\]</p><p>If distinct roots tend to the reference root, their section points and return times tend to \\(s_0\\) and \\(T\\); smooth flow dependence makes their entire orbit images tend to the reference image. Hence a nonisolated root does not give an isolated periodic orbit.</p><p>Conversely, if the root is isolated, shrink \\(S\\) so that \\(s_0\\) is its only return fixed point. The map\n\\[\n(s,\\tau)\\longmapsto\\Phi_\\tau(0,s)\n\\]\nis a local diffeomorphism near each \\((s_0,\\tau)\\), \\(0\\leq\\tau\\leq T\\). Finitely many flow neighborhoods cover the reference orbit and give an open neighborhood whose every point lies on a trajectory meeting \\(S\\). Any periodic orbit meeting it therefore meets \\(S\\). Its unique positive-axis intersection is a local return fixed point and equals \\(s_0\\); uniqueness makes it the reference orbit.</p><p>Every periodic orbit lies inside the open matching domain. A limiting endpoint at the equilibrium is not an additional periodic orbit. Nonisolated roots, including a zero interval and its endpoints within the domain, do not count as limit cycles; isolated multiple roots remain covered.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "Lemma 2.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(\\phi\\in C^\\infty((0,\\infty))\\cap C([0,\\infty))\\), with \\(\\phi(0)=0\\).", "statement": "<p>Let \\(\\phi\\in C^\\infty((0,\\infty))\\cap C([0,\\infty))\\), with \\(\\phi(0)=0\\). Derivatives of \\(\\phi\\) need not extend to zero. Then all boundary-coordinate assertions in Proposition 3 hold.</p>", "proof": "<p>At fixed peak, the height derivatives in Proposition 1 show that the lower endpoint decreases and the upper endpoint increases as \\(h\\downarrow0\\). They remain bounded. Indeed, put\n\\[\nB=\\sup_{0\\leq u\\leq t}|\\phi(u)|.\n\\]\nIf an upper endpoint lies beyond \\(B\\), integrate \\(u_y\\leq B-y\\) from \\(B\\) to that endpoint, using \\(u(B)\\leq t\\). This gives\n\\[\ny_&gt;\\leq B+\\sqrt{2t}.\n\\]\nSimilarly,\n\\[\ny_&lt;\\geq-B-\\sqrt{2t}.\n\\]\nThe remaining bounds follow from \\(y_&lt;&lt;\\phi(h)&lt;y_&gt;\\). Thus finite limits exist, and their signs follow from continuity at zero.</p><p>For \\(t_2&gt;t_1\\), endpoint order passes to\n\\[\na(t_2)\\leq a(t_1),\\qquad b(t_2)\\geq b(t_1).\n\\]\nHence \\(T_\\phi\\) is upward closed.</p><p>Fix \\(t_0\\in T_\\phi\\). At a sufficiently small positive level \\(\\delta&lt;t_0\\), continue the endpoint branches downwards by\n\\[\n\\frac{dy}{du}=\\frac1{\\phi(u)-y}=:g(u,y).\n\\]\nChoose compact neighborhoods of \\(a(t_0)\\) and \\(b(t_0)\\), and decrease \\(\\delta\\) if necessary. On the resulting branch rectangles, \\(|\\phi(u)-y|\\) is bounded away from zero for \\(0\\leq u\\leq\\delta\\). The function \\(g\\) is continuous in \\(u\\), smooth in \\(y\\), and has bounded derivatives of every fixed order with respect to \\(y\\).</p><p>At \\(u=\\delta\\), branch initial values depend smoothly on \\(t\\). The integral equation and parameter-variation equations show that the branches and all their \\(t\\)-derivatives are continuous down to zero, locally uniformly in \\(t\\). Explicitly,\n\\[\ny_t(u,t)=y_t(\\delta,t)\n\\exp\\left(\\int_\\delta^u g_y(v,y(v,t))\\,dv\\right).\n\\]\nThe exponential is positive and bounded away from zero. Thus the nonzero variation signs persist uniformly near \\(t_0\\). Higher derivatives satisfy linear equations with continuous bounded coefficients and forcing built from lower derivatives. The strict endpoint signs also persist nearby. This proves openness and smoothness. An open upward-closed subset of \\((0,\\infty)\\) is an open ray or is empty.</p><p>On this ray,\n\\[\nr_t=\\frac{b_t-a_t}{2}&gt;0.\n\\]\nPassing to the limit in \\(r^2\\geq2(t-h)\\) gives \\(r(0,t)^2\\geq2t\\), so the width tends to infinity with the peak. Its image is one open ray, and its inverse is smooth.</p><p>For convergence at fixed width, the preceding bounds give \\(r_t(h,t)\\geq c_0&gt;0\\) near \\(t_0\\), uniformly for sufficiently small \\(h\\geq0\\). Strict monotonicity brackets the inverse peak \\(t=T(h,r)\\) in a common compact interval for nearby widths. The inverse is continuous in \\((h,r)\\), smooth in \\(r\\), and\n\\[\nT_r=\\frac1{r_t(h,T(h,r))}.\n\\]\nHigher \\(r\\)-derivatives are rational expressions in continuous peak derivatives, with powers of \\(r_t\\) in the denominator. They converge locally uniformly as \\(h\\downarrow0\\). Composing the endpoint data with this inverse and subtracting \\(\\phi(h)\\) proves convergence of all midpoint \\(r\\)-derivatives. No derivative of \\(\\phi\\) at zero is needed. Strict endpoint signs give \\(|M|&lt;r\\), and opposite nonzero peak derivatives give \\(|M_r|&lt;1\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Lemmas 3.1 and 3.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The reference functions are smooth for \\(s>0\\), exist at every finite positive width, and satisfy \\( \\partial_sj_\\kappa=-s\\kappa\\operatorname{sech}^2\\Theta_\\kappa, \\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For \\(\\kappa,z\\in\\mathbb R\\), take the entire quadratic profile\n  \\[\n  zu+\\frac{\\kappa}{2}u^2\n  \\]\n  with peak height zero.</p><p class=\"li\">As the base moves downwards from the peak, let \\(s&gt;0\\) denote characteristic width and let\n  \\[\n  j_\\kappa(s,z),\\quad \\Theta_\\kappa(s,z),\\quad L_\\kappa(s,z)\n  \\]\n  be its base slope, \\(\\operatorname{arctanh}(M/s)\\), and \\(\\operatorname{arctanh}v\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(z=z(\\kappa,s,\\theta)\\) be the inverse of \\(z\\mapsto\\Theta_\\kappa(s,z)\\), and define\n  \\[\n  J_\\kappa(s,\\theta)=j_\\kappa(s,z(\\kappa,s,\\theta)).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The reference functions are smooth for \\(s&gt;0\\), exist at every finite positive width, and satisfy\n\\[\n\\partial_sj_\\kappa=-s\\kappa\\operatorname{sech}^2\\Theta_\\kappa,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\partial_s\\Theta_\\kappa=j_\\kappa-\\frac{\\sinh(2\\Theta_\\kappa)}s,\\qquad\n\\partial_sL_\\kappa=\\frac{\\sinh(2\\Theta_\\kappa)}s,\n\\]\nwith limiting values \\(j_\\kappa(0,z)=z\\) and \\(\\Theta_\\kappa(0,z)=L_\\kappa(0,z)=0\\). Moreover,\n\\[\n|j_\\kappa(s,z)-z|\\leq\\frac{|\\kappa|s^2}{2},\\qquad\n\\partial_zj_\\kappa&gt;0,\\qquad\n\\partial_z\\Theta_\\kappa&gt;0.\n\\]\nFor fixed \\((\\kappa,s)\\), \\(z\\mapsto\\Theta_\\kappa(s,z)\\) is a bijection onto \\(\\mathbb R\\), and\n\\[\n\\partial_zL_\\kappa(s,z)\n=\\int_0^s\\frac{2\\cosh(2\\Theta_\\kappa(q,z))}{q}\n\\,\\partial_z\\Theta_\\kappa(q,z)\\,dq&gt;0.\n\\]\nIn particular,\n\\[\n(J_\\kappa)_\\theta=\\frac{\\partial_zj_\\kappa}{\\partial_z\\Theta_\\kappa}&gt;0.\n\\]</p><p>For comparison, let \\(\\phi\\) be smooth on a neighborhood of an arc’s traversed height interval, and parametrize that arc from its peak \\(t\\) to final width \\(r\\) by \\(s\\in(0,r]\\). Write \\(h(s),\\theta(s),L(s)\\), set \\(k=\\phi'\\), and define\n\\[\nE(s)=k(h(s))-J_\\kappa(s,\\theta(s)),\\qquad\n\\theta(s)=\\Theta_\\kappa(s,z(s)).\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nz(s)\\to k(t),\\qquad E(s)\\to0\n\\]\nas \\(s\\downarrow0\\), and\n\\[\nE'+(J_\\kappa)_\\theta(s,\\theta(s))E\n=-s\\bigl(\\phi''(h(s))-\\kappa\\bigr)\\operatorname{sech}^2\\theta(s),\n\\]\n\\[\nz'=\\frac{E}{\\partial_z\\Theta_\\kappa(s,z(s))}.\n\\]\nWriting \\(a(s)=(J_\\kappa)_\\theta(s,\\theta(s))&gt;0\\) and \\(f(s)\\) for the displayed forcing,\n\\[\nE(s)=\\int_0^s\n\\exp\\left(-\\int_q^sa(\\xi)\\,d\\xi\\right)f(q)\\,dq.\n\\]</p><p>If \\(\\phi''(h(s))\\geq\\kappa\\) throughout the characteristic, then\n\\[\nE\\leq0,\\qquad z'\\leq0,\\qquad\n\\theta(s)\\geq\\Theta_\\kappa(s,z(r))\\quad(0&lt;s&lt;r),\n\\]\n\\[\nL(r)\\geq L_\\kappa(r,z(r)).\n\\]\nIf the curvature difference is not identically zero on \\((0,r)\\), the angle inequalities and the final \\(L\\)-inequality are strict, and \\(E(r)&lt;0\\). All signs reverse when \\(\\phi''(h(s))\\leq\\kappa\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 4.1 supplies the reference family and its smooth inverse. Along a reference characteristic, its equations give\n\\[\n(J_\\kappa)_s+(J_\\kappa)_\\theta\n\\left(J_\\kappa-\\frac{\\sinh(2\\theta)}s\\right)\n=-s\\kappa\\operatorname{sech}^2\\theta.\n\\]\nSubtracting this from the actual slope evolution proves the equation for \\(E\\). Differentiating\n\\[\n\\theta(s)=\\Theta_\\kappa(s,z(s))\n\\]\nand subtracting the angle equations gives the formula for \\(z'\\).</p><p>The small-width expansions give \\(\\theta(s)/s\\to k(t)/3\\). For each \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), the reference angles labelled \\(k(t)-\\varepsilon\\) and \\(k(t)+\\varepsilon\\) bracket the actual angle at sufficiently small widths. Reference monotonicity implies \\(z(s)\\to k(t)\\). The slope bound then gives \\(E(s)\\to0\\).</p><p>For \\(0&lt;\\varepsilon&lt;s\\), variation of constants yields\n\\[\nE(s)=E(\\varepsilon)\\exp\\left(-\\int_\\varepsilon^sa\\right)\n+\\int_\\varepsilon^s\\exp\\left(-\\int_q^sa\\right)f(q)\\,dq.\n\\]\nThe first term tends to zero because \\(a&gt;0\\) and \\(E(\\varepsilon)\\to0\\). Also \\(f(q)=O(q)\\) near the smooth peak, and the exponential lies between zero and one. Dominated convergence gives the from-zero formula without assuming that \\(a\\) is integrable at zero.</p><p>If the curvature difference is nonnegative, \\(f\\leq0\\), hence \\(E\\leq0\\) and \\(z'\\leq0\\). Reference monotonicity gives the weak angle inequalities. If the difference is positive somewhere, continuity gives an interior interval where \\(f&lt;0\\). The integral formula makes \\(E\\) strictly negative from the end of that interval through \\(r\\). Consequently \\(z(s)&gt;z(r)\\) for every \\(s&lt;r\\), giving strict angle inequalities. The integral formula for \\(L\\) in Proposition 2 gives the strict final \\(L\\)-inequality. Reversing the curvature inequality reverses all signs.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The quadratic reference functions in Proposition 4 have all the existence, smoothness, differential-equation, monotonicity, range, and \\(\\partial_zL_\\kappa\\) properties stated there.", "statement": "<p>The quadratic reference functions in Proposition 4 have all the existence, smoothness, differential-equation, monotonicity, range, and \\(\\partial_zL_\\kappa\\) properties stated there.</p>", "proof": "<p>The entire-profile construction of Proposition 1 gives an arc at every lower base height. Width tends to zero at the peak and to infinity as the base tends to \\(-\\infty\\), since \\(s^2\\geq2(t-h)\\). Its strictly negative base derivative gives smooth inversion from base height to any \\(s&gt;0\\), including smooth coefficient dependence. Thus no finite-width existence claim is inferred from the singular equations at zero. The characteristic equations of Proposition 1 give the displayed reference equations, and integration of the slope equation gives\n\\[\n|j_\\kappa(s,z)-z|\\leq|\\kappa|s^2/2.\n\\]</p><p>For \\(\\kappa\\neq0\\), consider the single profile \\(\\kappa u^2/2\\). Let \\(\\bar h(t,s)\\) be its base height for peak \\(t\\) and width \\(s\\). Its peak slope is \\(z=\\kappa t\\), and its base slope is \\(\\kappa\\bar h(t,s)\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\partial_zj_\\kappa(s,z)=\\partial_t\\bar h(t,s)&gt;0.\n\\]\nThis is valid for either sign of \\(\\kappa\\). For \\(\\kappa=0\\), \\(j_0(s,z)=z\\).</p><p>Proposition 2 gives\n\\[\n\\Theta_\\kappa(s,z)=\\frac z3s+O(s^2),\\qquad\n\\partial_z\\Theta_\\kappa(s,z)=\\frac s3+O(s^2).\n\\]\nDifferentiating the angle equation gives\n\\[\n\\partial_s(\\partial_z\\Theta_\\kappa)\n=\\partial_zj_\\kappa\n-\\frac{2\\cosh(2\\Theta_\\kappa)}s\\,\\partial_z\\Theta_\\kappa.\n\\]\nThe initial sign and positive forcing prove \\(\\partial_z\\Theta_\\kappa&gt;0\\) by the scalar integrating-factor formula.</p><p>Fix \\(r&gt;0\\). For \\(0&lt;s\\leq r\\),\n\\[\nj_\\kappa(s,z)\\geq z-\\frac{|\\kappa|r^2}{2}.\n\\]\nFor sufficiently large positive \\(z\\), the angle is initially positive and cannot cross zero downwards. Given \\(N&gt;0\\), on \\([r/2,r]\\), whenever \\(0\\leq\\Theta_\\kappa\\leq N\\),\n\\[\n\\partial_s\\Theta_\\kappa\n\\geq z-\\frac{|\\kappa|r^2}{2}-\\frac{2\\sinh(2N)}r.\n\\]\nFor large \\(z\\), this forces a crossing of \\(N\\) if it has not already occurred, and prohibits a downward crossing. Thus \\(\\Theta_\\kappa(r,z)\\to+\\infty\\) as \\(z\\to+\\infty\\). Applying the same argument to \\((-j,-\\Theta,-\\kappa)\\) gives the negative limit. Strict monotonicity proves the bijection.</p><p>Finally,\n\\[\nL_\\kappa(s,z)=\\int_0^s\\frac{\\sinh(2\\Theta_\\kappa(q,z))}{q}\\,dq.\n\\]\nThe smooth small-width expansions, including the locally uniform parameter derivative, bound the differentiated integrand near zero. Differentiation under the integral gives the asserted positive formula.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Theorem 3.6, with Lemmas 3.3–3.5 and the midpoint fit of Section 3.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every \\(r>0\\), \\(|M|<r\\), and \\(|v|<1\\), there is a unique finite \\((\\lambda,\\kappa)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\) such that \\( H(\\lambda,\\kappa,r)=M,\\qquad H_r(\\lambda,\\kappa,r)=v. \\) The map …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For the entire quadratic profile\n  \\[\n  q_{\\lambda,\\kappa}(u)=\\lambda u+\\frac{\\kappa}{2}u^2,\n  \\]\n  let \\(H(\\lambda,\\kappa,r)\\) be its midpoint at base height zero and width \\(r&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Subscripts on model functions are ordinary partial derivatives with other arguments fixed.</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  P=H_\\lambda,\\quad Q=H_\\kappa,\\quad\n  R=P_r,\\quad S=Q_r,\\quad G=PS-QR.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(r&gt;0\\), \\(|M|&lt;r\\), and \\(\\kappa\\in\\mathbb R\\), let\n  \\[\n  H(\\lambda_*(r,M,\\kappa),\\kappa,r)=M,\n  \\]\n  and define\n  \\[\n  \\widehat v(r,M,\\kappa)=H_r(\\lambda_*(r,M,\\kappa),\\kappa,r),\n  \\qquad\n  \\widehat L=\\operatorname{arctanh}\\widehat v.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(r&gt;0\\), \\(|M|&lt;r\\), and \\(|v|&lt;1\\), there is a unique finite \\((\\lambda,\\kappa)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\) such that\n\\[\nH(\\lambda,\\kappa,r)=M,\\qquad H_r(\\lambda,\\kappa,r)=v.\n\\]\nThe map\n\\[\n(\\lambda,\\kappa,r)\\longmapsto\n(H(\\lambda,\\kappa,r),H_r(\\lambda,\\kappa,r),r)\n\\]\nis a diffeomorphism from \\(\\mathbb R^2\\times(0,\\infty)\\) onto\n\\[\n\\{(M,v,r):r&gt;0,\\ |M|&lt;r,\\ |v|&lt;1\\}.\n\\]\nIn particular, fitted parameters converge to a finite pair whenever their data converge to an interior point, even when width varies.</p><p>At every model arc,\n\\[\nP,Q,R,S,G&gt;0,\\qquad |H|&lt;r,\\qquad |H_r|&lt;1.\n\\]\nThe midpoint fit \\(\\lambda_*\\) exists uniquely and is jointly smooth. Moreover,\n\\[\n\\partial_\\kappa\\widehat L&gt;0,\\qquad\n\\partial_\\kappa\\widehat v=\\frac GP&gt;0,\n\\]\nand, at fixed \\((r,M)\\),\n\\[\n\\widehat L\\longrightarrow\\pm\\infty\n\\quad\\text{as }\\kappa\\longrightarrow\\pm\\infty.\n\\]</p><p>The reflection identities are\n\\[\nH(-\\lambda,-\\kappa,r)=-H(\\lambda,\\kappa,r),\\qquad\nH_r(-\\lambda,-\\kappa,r)=-H_r(\\lambda,\\kappa,r).\n\\]\nThe width identities are\n\\[\n\\kappa P+\\lambda=\\frac{rH_r+H}{r^2-H^2},\n\\]\nand, with \\(L_{\\rm mod}=\\operatorname{arctanh}H_r\\),\n\\[\n\\kappa R=\n\\frac{r(1-H_r^2)}{r^2-H^2}\n\\left((L_{\\rm mod})_r-\\frac{2H}{r^2-H^2}\\right).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 1 gives \\(|H|&lt;r\\) and \\(|H_r|&lt;1\\). Lemma 5.1 gives a unique smooth slope fit for every curvature. Lemmas 5.1 and 5.2 show that its conditional \\(\\widehat L\\) is strictly increasing from \\(-\\infty\\) to \\(+\\infty\\). Thus exactly one curvature gives \\(\\widehat L=\\operatorname{arctanh}v\\), and then exactly one slope fits \\(M\\).</p><p>The signs follow from Lemmas 5.1 and 5.3. The Jacobian of the displayed three-dimensional map has last row \\((0,0,1)\\) and determinant \\(G&gt;0\\). The inverse function theorem gives smooth local inverses; uniqueness makes them agree on overlaps. Hence the inverse is globally smooth. Its continuity gives convergence to finite fitted parameters at interior limiting data.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "the midpoint-fit construction and Lemma 3.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In the setting of Proposition 5, \\( P>0,\\qquad Q>0. \\) For every \\(r>0\\), \\(|M|<r\\), and \\(\\kappa\\in\\mathbb R\\), the unique midpoint fit \\(\\lambda_*(r,M,\\kappa)\\) exists and is jointly smooth.", "statement": "<p>In the setting of Proposition 5,\n\\[\nP&gt;0,\\qquad Q&gt;0.\n\\]\nFor every \\(r&gt;0\\), \\(|M|&lt;r\\), and \\(\\kappa\\in\\mathbb R\\), the unique midpoint fit \\(\\lambda_*(r,M,\\kappa)\\) exists and is jointly smooth. At this fit,\n\\[\n\\partial_\\kappa\\widehat L&gt;0,\\qquad\n\\partial_\\kappa\\widehat v=\\frac GP&gt;0.\n\\]\nConsequently \\(G&gt;0\\) at every model arc.</p>", "proof": "<p>The profile variations \\(u\\) and \\(u^2/2\\) are strictly positive in every base-zero arc interior. The positive denominator in Proposition 2 therefore gives \\(P,Q&gt;0\\). Reflection of the endpoint coordinate \\(y\\) negates the profile and midpoint while preserving width, proving the reflection identities in Proposition 5.</p><p>For prescribed \\(r,M,\\kappa\\), Proposition 4 gives a unique peak label with\n\\[\n\\Theta_\\kappa(r,z)=\\operatorname{arctanh}(M/r).\n\\]\nTranslate that arc to base height zero and subtract the profile value at the base. Its base slope is\n\\[\n\\lambda=j_\\kappa(r,z),\n\\]\nits curvature is \\(\\kappa\\), and its midpoint is \\(M\\). Every base-zero quadratic arc arises this way. Since \\(P&gt;0\\), the midpoint fit is unique; the implicit function theorem makes it jointly smooth.</p><p>Fix \\(r,M,\\kappa\\). For \\(\\delta&gt;0\\), let \\(\\theta_\\delta\\) be the characteristic of the midpoint-fitted curvature-\\(\\kappa+\\delta\\) model, and let \\(\\theta_0\\) correspond to curvature \\(\\kappa\\). Their final angles agree. Proposition 4 gives\n\\[\n\\theta_\\delta(s)&gt;\\theta_0(s)\\qquad(0&lt;s&lt;r).\n\\]\nThus \\(\\widehat L\\) is strictly increasing. To prove a strictly positive derivative, compare the curvature-\\(\\kappa+\\delta\\) characteristic using \\(J_\\kappa\\). Its error satisfies\n\\[\nE_\\delta'+a_\\delta E_\\delta\n=-\\delta s\\operatorname{sech}^2\\theta_\\delta,\\qquad\na_\\delta=(J_\\kappa)_\\theta(s,\\theta_\\delta)&gt;0,\\qquad E_\\delta\\leq0.\n\\]</p><p>Choose \\(0&lt;a&lt;b&lt;c&lt;r\\). Smooth midpoint fitting and finite-parameter arc dependence give constants \\(A,q_0&gt;0\\), independent of sufficiently small \\(\\delta\\geq0\\), such that on \\([a,r]\\),\n\\[\na_\\delta\\leq A,\\qquad s\\operatorname{sech}^2\\theta_\\delta\\geq q_0.\n\\]\nVariation of constants from \\(a\\), using \\(E_\\delta(a)\\leq0\\), gives\n\\[\nE_\\delta(s)\\leq-c_0\\delta\\quad(b\\leq s\\leq r),\\qquad\nc_0=q_0(b-a)e^{-Ar}&gt;0.\n\\]</p><p>Define \\(z_\\delta(s)\\) by\n\\[\n\\theta_\\delta(s)=\\Theta_\\kappa(s,z_\\delta(s)),\n\\]\nand let \\(z_0\\) be the constant label of the curvature-\\(\\kappa\\) arc. Final matching gives \\(z_\\delta(r)=z_0\\). On the relevant compact set for \\(b\\leq s\\leq r\\), including intervals between these labels, there are \\(m_0,B_0&gt;0\\) with\n\\[\nm_0\\leq\\partial_z\\Theta_\\kappa\\leq B_0.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\nz_\\delta'(s)\\leq-\\frac{c_0\\delta}{B_0}.\n\\]\nIntegrating backwards gives, for \\(b\\leq s\\leq c\\),\n\\[\nz_\\delta(s)-z_0\\geq\\frac{c_0\\delta(r-c)}{B_0},\\qquad\n\\theta_\\delta(s)-\\theta_0(s)\n\\geq\\frac{m_0c_0\\delta(r-c)}{B_0}.\n\\]\nSince the derivative of \\(\\sinh(2\\theta)\\) is at least two, the \\(L\\)-integrals give\n\\[\n\\widehat L(r,M,\\kappa+\\delta)-\\widehat L(r,M,\\kappa)\n\\geq\n\\frac{2m_0c_0(r-c)}{B_0}\\log\\frac cb\\,\\delta.\n\\]\nSmoothness at final positive width proves \\(\\partial_\\kappa\\widehat L&gt;0\\). No differentiation of the improper \\(L\\)-integral is used.</p><p>Finally,\n\\[\n\\partial_\\kappa\\lambda_*=-\\frac QP,\n\\]\nso\n\\[\n\\partial_\\kappa\\widehat v\n=S-R\\frac QP=\\frac GP\n=(1-\\widehat v^2)\\partial_\\kappa\\widehat L&gt;0.\n\\]\nEvery model arc is its own midpoint fit, hence \\(G&gt;0\\) everywhere.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "Lemma 3.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For fixed \\(r>0\\) and \\(|M|<r\\), \\( \\lim_{\\kappa\\to+\\infty}\\widehat L(r,M,\\kappa)=+\\infty,\\qquad \\lim_{\\kappa\\to-\\infty}\\widehat L(r,M,\\kappa)=-\\infty. \\)", "statement": "<p>For fixed \\(r&gt;0\\) and \\(|M|&lt;r\\),\n\\[\n\\lim_{\\kappa\\to+\\infty}\\widehat L(r,M,\\kappa)=+\\infty,\\qquad\n\\lim_{\\kappa\\to-\\infty}\\widehat L(r,M,\\kappa)=-\\infty.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose the first limit fails. Strict monotonicity makes \\(\\widehat L\\) bounded above as \\(\\kappa\\to+\\infty\\). Choose \\(\\kappa_n\\uparrow+\\infty\\), all positive, and denote the matched-final-angle characteristics by \\(\\theta_n(s),j_n(s)\\), and their final \\(L\\)-values by \\(L_n\\). Proposition 4 gives pointwise increase of \\(\\theta_n(s)\\) for \\(0&lt;s&lt;r\\). Put\n\\[\nf_n(s)=\\frac{\\sinh(2\\theta_n(s))}{s}.\n\\]\nEach \\(f_n\\) is integrable at zero, and\n\\[\nf_n-f_1\\geq0,\\qquad\n\\int_0^r(f_n-f_1)\\,ds=L_n-L_1\\leq C.\n\\]\nMonotone convergence shows that the limiting \\(f_n\\), and therefore the limiting \\(\\theta_n\\), are finite almost everywhere.</p><p>Choose four such widths \\(0&lt;a&lt;b&lt;c&lt;d&lt;r\\). The angle equation gives\n\\[\nA_n:=\\int_a^bj_n(s)\\,ds\n=\\theta_n(b)-\\theta_n(a)+\\int_a^bf_n(s)\\,ds,\n\\]\n\\[\nB_n:=\\int_c^dj_n(s)\\,ds\n=\\theta_n(d)-\\theta_n(c)+\\int_c^df_n(s)\\,ds.\n\\]\nBoth sequences are bounded above and below, because the four angle sequences are bounded and every subinterval integral of \\(f_n-f_1\\) lies between zero and \\(C\\).</p><p>Choose \\(A_*,B_*\\) with \\(A_n\\leq A_*\\) and \\(B_n\\geq B_*\\). Since \\(j_n\\) is decreasing,\n\\[\nj_n(b)\\leq\\frac{A_*}{b-a},\\qquad\nj_n(c)\\geq\\frac{B_*}{d-c}.\n\\]\nThus \\(j_n(b)-j_n(c)\\) has a uniform upper bound. But\n\\[\nj_n(b)-j_n(c)\n=\\kappa_n\\int_b^cs\\operatorname{sech}^2\\theta_n(s)\\,ds.\n\\]\nThe integrand is bounded by \\(s\\) and converges almost everywhere to a strictly positive function. Dominated convergence gives a strictly positive limiting integral, so the right side tends to infinity, a contradiction.</p><p>Reflection and uniqueness give\n\\[\n\\lambda_*(r,-M,-\\kappa)=-\\lambda_*(r,M,\\kappa),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\widehat L(r,M,\\kappa)=-\\widehat L(r,-M,-\\kappa).\n\\]\nThe positive-curvature result for midpoint \\(-M\\) proves the negative-curvature limit.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.3", "orig": "Lemma 3.5", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every \\((\\lambda,\\kappa,r)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\times(0,\\infty)\\), \\( R>0,\\qquad S>0, \\) and the two width identities in Proposition 5 hold.", "statement": "<p>For every \\((\\lambda,\\kappa,r)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\times(0,\\infty)\\),\n\\[\nR&gt;0,\\qquad S&gt;0,\n\\]\nand the two width identities in Proposition 5 hold.</p>", "proof": "<p>At base height \\(h\\), translating the quadratic and subtracting its base value changes its slope to \\(\\lambda+\\kappa h\\) and leaves curvature unchanged. Its midpoint is\n\\[\nH(\\lambda+\\kappa h,\\kappa,r).\n\\]\nAt \\(h=0\\), the midpoint transport identity gives\n\\[\n\\kappa P-\\frac r{r^2-H^2}H_r\n=\\frac H{r^2-H^2}-\\lambda,\n\\]\nhence\n\\[\n\\kappa P+\\lambda=\\frac{rH_r+H}{r^2-H^2}.\n\\]</p><p>Put \\(D_0=r^2-H^2\\), \\(v=H_r\\). Differentiating at fixed \\((\\lambda,\\kappa)\\),\n\\[\n\\kappa R=\n\\frac{rv_r+2v}{D_0}\n-\\frac{2(r-Hv)(rv+H)}{D_0^2}\n=\n\\frac{r(1-v^2)}{D_0}\n\\left(\\frac{v_r}{1-v^2}-\\frac{2H}{D_0}\\right).\n\\]\nThis is the second width identity.</p><p>Let \\(t=t(\\lambda,\\kappa,r)&gt;0\\) be the peak. Proposition 1 gives \\(t_r&gt;0\\). Its peak slope is \\(z=\\lambda+\\kappa t\\), so\n\\[\nL_{\\rm mod}(\\lambda,\\kappa,r)\n=L_\\kappa(r,\\lambda+\\kappa t).\n\\]\nThe chain rule gives\n\\[\n(L_{\\rm mod})_r=\\frac{2H}{r^2-H^2}\n+\\kappa t_r\\partial_zL_\\kappa(r,z).\n\\]\nFor \\(\\kappa\\neq0\\), substitution and cancellation yield\n\\[\nR=\\frac{r(1-H_r^2)}{r^2-H^2}\\,\nt_r\\partial_zL_\\kappa(r,z)&gt;0.\n\\]\nThe cancellation is valid for either sign of \\(\\kappa\\). For \\(\\kappa=0\\),\n\\[\nR=\\partial_\\lambda H_r\n=(1-H_r^2)\\partial_zL_0(r,\\lambda)&gt;0.\n\\]\nFinally,\n\\[\nS=\\frac{G+QR}{P}&gt;0.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Lemma 3.7", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The fitted parameters satisfy \\( P\\lambda_r+Q\\kappa_r=0, \\) \\( D\\kappa=-\\frac RG(\\lambda-k(h)), \\qquad D\\lambda-\\kappa=\\frac SG(\\lambda-k(h)). \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\phi\\in C^\\infty((0,\\infty))\\), \\(k=\\phi'\\), and let \\(M(h,r)\\), \\(v(h,r)=M_r(h,r)\\) be its arc data.</p><p class=\"li\">Define the unique fitted parameters by Proposition 5:\n  \\[\n  M=H(\\lambda(h,r),\\kappa(h,r),r),\\qquad\n  v=H_r(\\lambda(h,r),\\kappa(h,r),r).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Evaluate \\(P,Q,R,S,G\\) at this fit, and set\n  \\[\n  \\alpha=\\frac r{r^2-M^2},\\qquad D=\\partial_h-\\alpha\\partial_r.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The fitted parameters satisfy\n\\[\nP\\lambda_r+Q\\kappa_r=0,\n\\]\n\\[\nD\\kappa=-\\frac RG(\\lambda-k(h)),\n\\qquad\nD\\lambda-\\kappa=\\frac SG(\\lambda-k(h)).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Differentiate the midpoint fitting identity at fixed \\(h\\), and use the derivative fitting identity. This gives\n\\[\nP\\lambda_r+Q\\kappa_r=0.\n\\]</p><p>For a quadratic profile, raising the base changes slope at rate \\(\\kappa\\), leaves curvature fixed, and changes width at rate \\(-\\alpha\\). Proposition 1 therefore gives, at the fitted data,\n\\[\n\\kappa P-\\alpha H_r=\\frac M{r^2-M^2}-\\lambda,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\kappa R-\\alpha H_{rr}\n=-\\frac{2Mr(1-v^2)}{(r^2-M^2)^2}.\n\\]\nFor the actual profile,\n\\[\nDM=P\\,D\\lambda+Q\\,D\\kappa-\\alpha H_r,\n\\]\n\\[\nDv=R\\,D\\lambda+S\\,D\\kappa-\\alpha H_{rr}.\n\\]\nThe expression for \\(Dv\\) depends only on \\(M,v,r\\), so it agrees for the matched data. Subtraction yields\n\\[\nP(D\\lambda-\\kappa)+QD\\kappa=\\lambda-k(h),\n\\]\n\\[\nR(D\\lambda-\\kappa)+SD\\kappa=0.\n\\]\nTheir determinant is \\(G&gt;0\\). Solving proves the transport equations.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Theorem 4.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every \\(\\lambda,\\kappa\\in\\mathbb R\\) and \\(r>0\\), \\( \\left(\\frac RG\\right)_r\\leq0. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(H(\\lambda,\\kappa,r)\\) be the quadratic midpoint model of Proposition 5.</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  P=H_\\lambda,\\quad Q=H_\\kappa,\\quad R=P_r,\\quad S=Q_r,\\quad G=PS-QR.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Throughout this proposition, \\(r\\)-subscripts are partial derivatives with \\(\\lambda,\\kappa\\) fixed.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal W=RS_r-SR_r,\\qquad\n  \\alpha=\\frac r{r^2-H^2},\\qquad\n  \\gamma=\\frac{r^2+H^2+2rHH_r}{(r^2-H^2)^2},\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal D=\\kappa\\partial_\\lambda-\\alpha\\partial_r.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(\\lambda,\\kappa\\in\\mathbb R\\) and \\(r&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac RG\\right)_r\\leq0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Assume first \\(\\kappa&gt;0\\). Every model data point belongs to a characteristic from a finite peak, by Propositions 1 and 4. Parametrize it by outward width \\(s\\). Its base slope tends to a finite peak slope as \\(s\\downarrow0\\), so Lemma 7.1 makes \\(\\mathcal W&gt;0\\) at sufficiently small widths.</p><p>Along it, \\(\\kappa\\) is constant and \\(d\\lambda/ds=-\\kappa/\\alpha\\). Thus\n\\[\n\\frac d{ds}\\mathcal W(\\lambda(s),\\kappa,s)\n=\\mathcal W_r-\\frac{\\kappa}{\\alpha}\\mathcal W_\\lambda\n=-\\frac1\\alpha\\mathcal D\\mathcal W.\n\\]\nAt any zero, Lemmas 7.1 and 7.4 give\n\\[\n\\mathcal D\\mathcal W=-G(\\gamma_{rr}-\\sigma_0\\gamma_r)&lt;0.\n\\]\nThe outward derivative is therefore strictly positive. A first zero reached from positive values would have nonpositive outward derivative, a contradiction. Hence \\(\\mathcal W&gt;0\\) for \\(\\kappa&gt;0\\).</p><p>The reflection identity of Proposition 5 shows that \\(P,Q\\), their \\(r\\)-derivatives, and \\(\\mathcal W\\) are unchanged under \\((\\lambda,\\kappa)\\mapsto(-\\lambda,-\\kappa)\\). Therefore \\(\\mathcal W&gt;0\\) also for \\(\\kappa&lt;0\\). Smooth dependence at finite parameters gives \\(\\mathcal W\\geq0\\) at \\(\\kappa=0\\).</p><p>Finally,\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac RG\\right)_r=-\\frac{P\\mathcal W}{G^2}\\leq0.\n\\]\nThe characteristic argument establishes the sign of a scalar function on the whole model domain; it does not identify its partial \\(r\\)-derivative with a characteristic derivative.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "Section 4.1, Equations (4.3)–(4.12)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "In the setting of Proposition 7, \\( G_r=PS_r-QR_r,\\qquad \\left(\\frac RG\\right)_r=-\\frac{P\\mathcal W}{G^2}, \\) and \\( \\mathcal D\\mathcal W =(2\\gamma+3\\alpha_r)\\mathcal W+\\gamma_rG_r-\\gamma_{rr}G. \\) Locally uniformly for …", "statement": "<p>In the setting of Proposition 7,\n\\[\nG_r=PS_r-QR_r,\\qquad\n\\left(\\frac RG\\right)_r=-\\frac{P\\mathcal W}{G^2},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\mathcal D\\mathcal W\n=(2\\gamma+3\\alpha_r)\\mathcal W+\\gamma_rG_r-\\gamma_{rr}G.\n\\]\nLocally uniformly for finite \\((\\lambda,\\kappa)\\),\n\\[\n\\mathcal W=\\frac{16}{45}r^3+O(r^4)\n\\quad\\text{as }r\\downarrow0.\n\\]\nAt a zero of \\(\\mathcal W\\), setting \\(\\sigma_0=R_r/R\\), one has\n\\[\nR_r=\\sigma_0R,\\qquad S_r=\\sigma_0S,\\qquad G_r=\\sigma_0G,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\mathcal D\\mathcal W=-G(\\gamma_{rr}-\\sigma_0\\gamma_r).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Since \\(P_r=R\\) and \\(Q_r=S\\), direct differentiation gives the first two identities.</p><p>The quadratic midpoint transport identity is\n\\[\n\\mathcal DH=\\frac H{r^2-H^2}-\\lambda.\n\\]\nDifferentiating in \\(\\lambda\\) and \\(\\kappa\\), including derivatives of the operator \\(\\mathcal D\\), gives\n\\[\n\\mathcal DP=\\gamma P-1,\\qquad\n\\mathcal DQ=\\gamma Q-P.\n\\]\nFor example, differentiating in \\(\\lambda\\) contributes \\(-\\alpha_\\lambda H_r\\) on the left, where\n\\[\n\\alpha_\\lambda=\\frac{2rHP}{(r^2-H^2)^2}.\n\\]\nDifferentiating in \\(\\kappa\\) also contributes \\(P\\), because the coefficient of \\(\\partial_\\lambda\\) is \\(\\kappa\\).</p><p>The commutator\n\\[\n\\mathcal D(f_r)=(\\mathcal Df)_r+\\alpha_rf_r\n\\]\ngives\n\\[\n\\mathcal DR=(\\gamma+\\alpha_r)R+\\gamma_rP,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathcal DS=(\\gamma+\\alpha_r)S+\\gamma_rQ-R,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathcal D(R_r)\n=(\\gamma+2\\alpha_r)R_r+(2\\gamma_r+\\alpha_{rr})R+\\gamma_{rr}P,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathcal D(S_r)\n=(\\gamma+2\\alpha_r)S_r+(2\\gamma_r+\\alpha_{rr})S+\\gamma_{rr}Q-R_r.\n\\]\nApplying these formulas to \\(RS_r-SR_r\\), the \\(\\alpha_{rr}\\)-terms cancel, giving the stated transport equation.</p><p>The variation formula and smooth scaling of Proposition 2 give\n\\[\nP=\\frac{r^2}{3}+O(r^3),\\quad\nR=\\frac{2r}{3}+O(r^2),\\quad\nR_r=\\frac23+O(r),\n\\]\n\\[\nQ=\\frac{r^4}{15}+O(r^5),\\quad\nS=\\frac{4r^3}{15}+O(r^4),\\quad\nS_r=\\frac{4r^2}{5}+O(r^3).\n\\]\nAfter width-one scaling, the model parameters are \\(\\lambda r,\\kappa r^3\\), the limiting arc is a regular parabola, and the first variations in \\(u,u^2/2\\) are \\(1/3,1/15\\). Smooth dependence permits the displayed differentiations, uniformly on compact parameter sets. Substitution gives\n\\[\n\\mathcal W=\\frac{16}{45}r^3+O(r^4).\n\\]</p><p>At a zero, \\(R&gt;0\\) permits \\(\\sigma_0=R_r/R\\). The equation \\(\\mathcal W=0\\) gives \\(S_r=\\sigma_0S\\), and then \\(G_r=\\sigma_0G\\). The final identity follows from the transport equation.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.2", "orig": "Lemma 4.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "One has \\( X_mZ_{mm}-Z_mX_{mm} =\\frac{\\ell^2(m+n)^2}{m^2n^2(1+\\ell)^3}D_* =\\frac{3\\kappa^2}{m_r^3}\\mathcal W. \\) At a zero of \\(\\mathcal W\\) with \\(m\\neq n\\), the quantity \\( \\sigma=\\frac{X_{mm}}{X_m} \\) is well …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain Proposition 7, and assume \\(\\kappa=2b&gt;0\\), \\(\\lambda=d\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The quadratic profile is \\(du+bu^2\\), with base-zero endpoints \\(-m,n\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  m=r-H,\\qquad n=r+H,\\qquad\n  \\ell=\\frac{dn}{dm}=\\frac{1+H_r}{1-H_r}&gt;0,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  w=\\frac{\\ell_m}{\\ell},\\qquad\n  \\mathscr S=\\frac{\\ell_{mm}}{\\ell}-\\frac32w^2,\\qquad\n  w_m=\\mathscr S+\\frac12w^2.\n  \\]\n  Here \\(m_r=1-H_r&gt;0\\), so \\(m\\) is a valid local coordinate at fixed \\((d,b)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  X=\\frac{\\ell/m-1/n}{1+\\ell}=\\kappa P+d,\\qquad\n  Z=\\frac{\\ell m-n}{1+\\ell}=dP+3\\kappa Q,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  \\gamma=\\frac{\\ell/m^2+1/n^2}{1+\\ell},\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  D_*=(m-n)\\mathscr S+\n  \\left[1-\\frac{2n}{m}+\\ell\\left(\\frac{2m}{n}-1\\right)\\right]w\n  +\\frac{m+n}{2}w^2,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  w_*=\n  \\frac{2(\\ell m^4-2\\ell m^3n+2mn^3-n^4)}\n       {m^2n^2(m+n)}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>One has\n\\[\nX_mZ_{mm}-Z_mX_{mm}\n=\\frac{\\ell^2(m+n)^2}{m^2n^2(1+\\ell)^3}D_*\n=\\frac{3\\kappa^2}{m_r^3}\\mathcal W.\n\\]\nAt a zero of \\(\\mathcal W\\) with \\(m\\neq n\\), the quantity\n\\[\n\\sigma=\\frac{X_{mm}}{X_m}\n\\]\nis well defined, and\n\\[\n\\gamma_{rr}-\\sigma_0\\gamma_r\n=m_r^2(\\gamma_{mm}-\\sigma\\gamma_m),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\gamma_{mm}-\\sigma\\gamma_m\n=\\frac{\\ell(m+n)^2}{(1+\\ell)m^2n^2}\n\\frac{w_*-w}{m-n}.\n\\]\nAll displayed prefactors are strictly positive.</p>", "proof": "<p>The identity for \\(X\\) follows from the first width identity of Proposition 5. Width scaling gives\n\\[\nH(\\lambda,\\kappa,r)=rH(\\lambda r,\\kappa r^3,1),\\qquad\nrH_r-H=\\lambda P+3\\kappa Q.\n\\]\nIts endpoint form gives \\(Z\\). Substitution of \\(r=(m+n)/2\\), \\(H=(n-m)/2\\) in the definition of \\(\\gamma\\) gives its endpoint form.</p><p>Put\n\\[\nB=\\frac1{n^2}-\\frac1{m^2}.\n\\]\nDifferentiation, using \\(n_m=\\ell\\), gives\n\\[\nZ_m=\\frac{w\\ell(m+n)}{(1+\\ell)^2},\\qquad\nX_m=\\frac{\\ell B}{1+\\ell}+\\frac{Z_m}{mn},\\qquad\nB_m=\\frac2{m^3}-\\frac{2\\ell}{n^3}.\n\\]\nDifferentiate the middle identity and insert the first into the determinant. After division by \\(\\ell^2(m+n)/(1+\\ell)^3\\), the result is\n\\[\nB\\mathscr S+\n\\left(\\frac{B(1+\\ell)}{m+n}-B_m\\right)w\n+\\frac{(m+n)^2}{2m^2n^2}w^2.\n\\]\nThe derivatives used here are\n\\[\n\\frac d{dm}\\log\\frac{\\ell(m+n)}{(1+\\ell)^2}\n=\\frac{1-\\ell}{1+\\ell}w+\\frac{1+\\ell}{m+n},\n\\qquad\n\\left(\\frac1{mn}\\right)_m=-\\frac{n+m\\ell}{m^2n^2},\n\\]\ntogether with \\(w_m=\\mathscr S+w^2/2\\). Since\n\\[\nB=\\frac{(m-n)(m+n)}{m^2n^2},\n\\]\nthe coefficient of \\(w\\) reduces to\n\\[\n\\frac{m+n}{m^2n^2}\n\\left[1-\\frac{2n}{m}+\\ell\\left(\\frac{2m}{n}-1\\right)\\right].\n\\]\nThis proves the first determinant factorization.</p><p>Differentiating \\(X=\\kappa P+d\\), \\(Z=dP+3\\kappa Q\\) with respect to \\(r\\) gives\n\\[\nX_rZ_{rr}-Z_rX_{rr}=3\\kappa^2\\mathcal W.\n\\]\nThe chain rule gives\n\\[\nX_rZ_{rr}-Z_rX_{rr}\n=m_r^3(X_mZ_{mm}-Z_mX_{mm}),\n\\]\nproving the second factorization. Also\n\\[\nX_m=\\frac{\\kappa R}{m_r}&gt;0.\n\\]\nAt a zero, \\(\\sigma=X_{mm}/X_m\\) is legitimate, and the determinant gives \\(Z_{mm}=\\sigma Z_m\\). From \\(X_{rr}=\\sigma_0X_r\\),\n\\[\n\\sigma m_r^2=\\sigma_0m_r-m_{rr}.\n\\]\nThe chain rule for \\(\\gamma\\) gives the first asserted reduction.</p><p>For a function \\(f\\) smooth near \\(m\\) and \\(-n\\), put\n\\[\nTf=\\frac{\\ell f(m)+f(-n)}{1+\\ell},\\qquad\na_0=\\frac{\\ell}{1+\\ell},\\qquad\n\\beta=\\frac w{1+\\ell},\\qquad\n\\rho=(\\log a_0)_m-\\sigma.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n(Tf)_m\n=a_0\\{f'(m)-f'(-n)+\\beta[f(m)-f(-n)]\\},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{(Tf)_{mm}-\\sigma(Tf)_m}{a_0}\n={}&amp;f''(m)+\\ell f''(-n)\n+\\beta\\{f'(m)+\\ell f'(-n)\\}\\\\\n&amp;+\\rho\\{f'(m)-f'(-n)\\}\n+(\\beta_m+\\rho\\beta)\\{f(m)-f(-n)\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nAt the zero, this vanishes for \\(f(x)=x\\) and \\(f(x)=1/x\\), since \\(Tx=Z\\), \\(T_{1/x}=X\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\beta_m+\\rho\\beta=-\\frac{\\beta(1+\\ell)}{m+n},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\rho B=\\frac{2\\ell}{n^3}-\\frac2{m^3}\n+\\beta\\left(\\frac1{m^2}+\\frac\\ell{n^2}+\\frac{1+\\ell}{mn}\\right).\n\\]\nHere \\(B\\neq0\\), and no division by \\(\\beta\\) is made.</p><p>For \\(f(x)=1/x^2\\), the same formula gives\n\\[\n\\frac{\\gamma_{mm}-\\sigma\\gamma_m}{a_0}\n=\\frac6{m^4}+\\frac{6\\ell}{n^4}\n+\\beta\\left(-\\frac2{m^3}+\\frac{2\\ell}{n^3}\\right)\n-2\\rho\\left(\\frac1{m^3}+\\frac1{n^3}\\right)\n+\\frac{\\beta(1+\\ell)B}{m+n}.\n\\]\nEliminating \\(\\rho\\), the constant and \\(\\beta\\)-coefficients reduce to\n\\[\nA_0=\\frac{2(m+n)N}{m^4n^4(m-n)},\\qquad\nA_\\beta=-\\frac{(1+\\ell)(m+n)^2}{m^2n^2(m-n)},\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nN=\\ell m^4-2\\ell m^3n+2mn^3-n^4.\n\\]\nThe numerator reductions are\n\\[\n6(n^4+\\ell m^4)(m^2-n^2)\n-4(m^3+n^3)(\\ell m^3-n^3)\n=2(m+n)^2N,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(m+n)\\bigl[&amp;\n2(\\ell m^3-n^3)(m-n)\n-2(m^3+n^3)(\\ell m+n)\\\\\n&amp;+(1+\\ell)mn(m-n)^2\\bigr]\n=-(1+\\ell)mn(m+n)^3.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nSubstituting \\(\\beta=w/(1+\\ell)\\) gives\n\\[\nA_0+\\beta A_\\beta\n=\\frac{(m+n)^2}{m^2n^2}\\frac{w_*-w}{m-n}.\n\\]\nMultiplication by \\(a_0\\) proves the final formula.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.3", "orig": "Lemma 4.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For the quadratic profile \\(du+bu^2\\), \\(b>0\\), with the endpoint notation of Lemma 7.2 …", "statement": "<p>For the quadratic profile \\(du+bu^2\\), \\(b&gt;0\\), with the endpoint notation of Lemma 7.2,\n\\[\n\\mathscr S\n=2b(m+n\\ell^2)+\\frac{d^2}{2}(\\ell^2-1)\n-d\\left(\\frac1m+\\frac{\\ell^2}{n}\\right)\n+\\frac32\\left(\\frac{\\ell^2}{n^2}-\\frac1{m^2}\\right).\n\\]\nThe arc admits a positive function \\(V\\) such that\n\\[\n\\ell=\\frac{mV(-m)^2}{nV(n)^2},\n\\]\n\\[\nw=\\frac1m-\\frac\\ell n+d(1+\\ell)\n+2bmV(-m)^2\\int_{-m}^nV(y)^{-2}\\,dy.\n\\]\nIf \\(d&lt;0\\) and \\(d^2\\geq4bm\\), set\n\\[\n\\nu=\\sqrt{d^2/4-bm}.\n\\]\nThen, including \\(\\nu=0\\),\n\\[\nw\\leq\\frac1m-\\frac\\ell n+d\\ell-2\\nu.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>On the compact arc interval \\([-m,n]\\), define\n\\[\nV(y)=\\exp\\left[-\\int_{-m}^y(bu(s)+d/2)\\,ds\\right]&gt;0,\\qquad\nU(y)=u(y)V(y).\n\\]\nThe arc is bounded, so this is finite. Direct differentiation gives the regular linear system\n\\[\nU'=\\frac d2U-yV,\\qquad\nV'=-bU-\\frac d2V,\n\\]\nwith \\(U(-m)=U(n)=0\\). Eliminating \\(V\\) where \\(y\\neq0\\),\n\\[\nU''=\\frac{U'}y+\\left(by+\\frac{d^2}{4}-\\frac d{2y}\\right)U.\n\\]</p><p>Take \\((U_1,V_1)=(U,V)\\), and complete it to a fundamental pair of the regular system. Its matrix has trace zero, so\n\\[\n\\Omega=U_1V_2-U_2V_1\n\\]\nis a nonzero constant. At both endpoints, \\(U_1=0\\), \\(V_1&gt;0\\), hence \\(U_2=-\\Omega/V_1\\neq0\\). The same ratio \\(f=U_1/U_2\\) is therefore finite near both endpoints, and\n\\[\nf(-m)=f(n)=0,\\qquad\nf'(y)=\\frac{y\\Omega}{U_2(y)^2}\\neq0\n\\]\nat \\(y=-m,n\\).</p><p>A nearby arc has coefficients normalized as \\((1,-q)\\) in this fixed fundamental pair, because its first coefficient stays nonzero. Its endpoint condition is \\(U_1-qU_2=0\\). Thus nearby endpoints satisfy\n\\[\nf(n(m))=f(-m).\n\\]\nNo scalar solution is continued through the singular scalar equation at zero. The scalar determinant \\(U_1'U_2-U_1U_2'=y\\Omega\\) is nonzero near each endpoint.</p><p>Writing \\(U=\\sqrt{|y|}\\,\\widetilde U\\) gives\n\\[\n\\widetilde U''\n=\\left(by+\\frac{d^2}{4}-\\frac d{2y}+\\frac3{4y^2}\\right)\\widetilde U.\n\\]\nFor two solutions of \\(a''=Q_0a\\), \\(c''=Q_0c\\), their ratio has Schwarzian \\(-2Q_0\\): locally,\n\\[\n\\frac{(a/c)''}{(a/c)'}=-2\\frac{c'}c,\n\\]\nand differentiating and subtracting half its square gives \\(-2c''/c\\). Thus\n\\[\n\\{f,y\\}=-2by-\\frac{d^2}{2}+\\frac dy-\\frac3{2y^2}.\n\\]\nThe Schwarzian chain rule applied to \\(f(n(m))=f(-m)\\) gives\n\\[\n\\mathscr S=\\{f,y\\}_{y=-m}-\\ell^2\\{f,y\\}_{y=n}.\n\\]\nExpansion proves the formula for \\(\\mathscr S\\).</p><p>Varying \\(u(-m;m)=0\\), with \\(u_y(-m)=m\\), gives initial variation \\(u_m(-m)=m\\). Its scalar variational equation yields\n\\[\nu_m(y)=m\\exp\\left[\\int_{-m}^y(d+2bu(s))\\,ds\\right]\n=m\\frac{V(-m)^2}{V(y)^2}.\n\\]\nDifferentiating \\(u(n(m);m)=0\\), and using \\(u_y(n)=-n\\), gives the formula for \\(\\ell\\). Its logarithm is\n\\[\n\\log\\ell=\\log m-\\log n+\\int_{-m}^n(d+2bu(y))\\,dy.\n\\]\nDifferentiating in \\(m\\), the \\(2bu\\) endpoint contributions vanish and the constant contributes \\(d(1+\\ell)\\). Substitution of \\(u_m\\) gives the formula for \\(w\\).</p><p>Finally,\n\\[\nV''=(by+d^2/4)V,\\qquad V'(-m)=-dV(-m)/2.\n\\]\nFor \\(s=y+m\\), let \\(v_0(s)=V(-m+s)/V(-m)\\). Under the last assumptions,\n\\[\nv_0''=(\\nu^2+bs)v_0,\\qquad v_0(0)=1,\\qquad v_0'(0)=-d/2.\n\\]\nDefine\n\\[\nf_\\nu(s)=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\cosh(\\nu s)-\\dfrac d{2\\nu}\\sinh(\\nu s),&amp;\\nu&gt;0,\\\\\n1-ds/2,&amp;\\nu=0.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nVariation of constants gives\n\\[\nv_0(s)-f_\\nu(s)\n=b\\int_0^sK_\\nu(s-t)t\\,v_0(t)\\,dt\\geq0,\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nK_\\nu(t)=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\sinh(\\nu t)/\\nu,&amp;\\nu&gt;0,\\\\\nt,&amp;\\nu=0.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nThis uses the constructed positivity of \\(v_0\\); also \\(f_\\nu&gt;0\\) because \\(d&lt;0\\). Therefore\n\\[\nV(-m)^2\\int_{-m}^nV(y)^{-2}\\,dy\n\\leq\\int_0^\\infty f_\\nu(s)^{-2}\\,ds\n=\\frac1{\\nu-d/2}.\n\\]\nFor \\(\\nu&gt;0\\), the substitution \\(q=\\tanh(\\nu s)\\), \\(A=-d/(2\\nu)&gt;0\\), gives\n\\[\n\\frac1\\nu\\int_0^1(1+Aq)^{-2}\\,dq\n=\\frac1{\\nu(1+A)}.\n\\]\nFor \\(\\nu=0\\), direct integration gives \\(-2/d\\), the same formula. Since\n\\[\n\\frac{2bm}{\\nu-d/2}=-d-2\\nu,\n\\]\nsubstitution in the formula for \\(w\\) proves the estimate.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.4", "orig": "Lemma 4.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "If \\(\\kappa>0\\) and \\(\\mathcal W=0\\), then, with \\(\\sigma_0=R_r/R\\), \\( \\gamma_{rr}-\\sigma_0\\gamma_r>0. \\)", "statement": "<p>If \\(\\kappa&gt;0\\) and \\(\\mathcal W=0\\), then, with \\(\\sigma_0=R_r/R\\),\n\\[\n\\gamma_{rr}-\\sigma_0\\gamma_r&gt;0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the notation of Lemmas 7.2 and 7.3, with \\(d=\\lambda\\), \\(b=\\kappa/2&gt;0\\).</p><p>**Case 1: \\(m\\leq n\\).** The final angle \\(\\theta=\\operatorname{arctanh}(H/r)\\) is nonnegative. Along the characteristic,\n\\[\nj'=-s\\kappa\\operatorname{sech}^2\\theta&lt;0.\n\\]\nIts initial slope \\(z\\) must be positive. If \\(z\\leq0\\), then \\(j(s)&lt;0\\) for all \\(s&gt;0\\), and\n\\[\n\\theta'+a(s)\\theta=j(s),\\qquad\na(s)=\\frac{\\sinh(2\\theta)}{s\\theta}&gt;0,\n\\]\nwith value \\(2/s\\) at \\(\\theta=0\\), forces \\(\\theta(s)&lt;0\\). This follows by integration from \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), then \\(\\varepsilon\\downarrow0\\), since \\(\\theta(\\varepsilon)\\to0\\) and the forward homogeneous factor is at most one.</p><p>Thus \\(z&gt;0\\), and \\(\\theta(s)=zs/3+O(s^2)&gt;0\\) initially. If it first reached zero at \\(s_1&lt;r\\), then \\(j(s_1)=\\theta'(s_1)\\leq0\\); strict decrease of \\(j\\) forces \\(\\theta&lt;0\\) thereafter, contradicting the final angle. Hence \\(\\theta(s)&gt;0\\) for \\(0&lt;s&lt;r\\), and\n\\[\nL(r)=\\int_0^r\\frac{\\sinh(2\\theta(s))}{s}\\,ds&gt;0,\\qquad\n\\ell=e^{2L}&gt;1.\n\\]</p><p>For the fixed profile, its peak \\(t(r)\\) has \\(t_r&gt;0\\), and peak slope \\(z=d+\\kappa t\\). Proposition 4 gives\n\\[\nL_r\\big|_{d,\\kappa}\n=\\frac{\\sinh(2\\theta)}r+\\kappa t_r\\partial_zL_\\kappa(r,z)&gt;0,\n\\]\nso\n\\[\nw=\\frac{2L_r|_{d,\\kappa}}{m_r}&gt;0.\n\\]\nIf \\(m=n\\),\n\\[\nD_*=(\\ell-1)w+mw^2&gt;0,\n\\]\nso the determinant identity excludes a zero of \\(\\mathcal W\\).</p><p>If \\(m&lt;n\\), put \\(z_1=m/n\\in(0,1)\\). The sign of \\(w_*\\) is the sign of\n\\[\n\\ell z_1^3(z_1-2)+2z_1-1\n\\leq z_1^3(z_1-2)+2z_1-1\n=(z_1-1)^3(z_1+1)&lt;0.\n\\]\nThus \\((w_*-w)/(m-n)&gt;0\\), and Lemma 7.2 gives the required sign.</p><p>**Case 2: \\(m&gt;n\\) and \\(\\ell\\geq1\\).** The final angle is negative, forcing \\(d&lt;0\\): if the final driving slope \\(d\\) were nonnegative, its strict decrease would make it positive at all earlier widths, and the scalar angle equation would give a positive final angle.</p><p>Every group in the formula for \\(\\mathscr S\\) in Lemma 7.3 is now nonnegative, and several are strictly positive. Hence\n\\[\n\\mathscr S&gt;0.\n\\]\nWe show \\(w\\geq0\\) without assuming that \\(m\\) ranges to infinity. The fixed profile has arcs at every \\(r&gt;0\\). Since \\(m_r&gt;0\\) and \\(0&lt;m(r)&lt;2r\\), its \\(m\\)-domain is \\((0,M_*)\\), with \\(M_*\\) finite or infinite. Near its left end,\n\\[\n\\ell=1+\\frac43dr+O(r^2)&lt;1.\n\\]\nFix a point in \\(\\{m-n&gt;0,\\ell&gt;1\\}\\), and let \\((a,c)\\) be its connected component. Then \\(a&gt;0\\) and \\(a&lt;M_*\\). On the component,\n\\[\n(m-n)_m=1-\\ell&lt;0.\n\\]\nMoving backwards to \\(a\\) increases \\(m-n\\), so the component cannot enter through \\(m=n\\). It has \\(\\ell(a)=1\\), with \\(\\ell_m(a)\\geq0\\), hence \\(w(a)\\geq0\\). Since\n\\[\nw_m=\\mathscr S+w^2/2&gt;0,\n\\]\none has \\(w&gt;0\\) inside.</p><p>At a point with \\(m&gt;n,\\ell=1,w&lt;0\\), points immediately left would have \\(\\ell&gt;1,w&lt;0\\), a contradiction. If \\(w=0\\) there, then \\(w_m=\\mathscr S&gt;0\\) and\n\\[\n\\ell_{mm}=\\ell(w_m+w^2)=\\mathscr S&gt;0,\n\\]\nagain giving \\(\\ell&gt;1,w&lt;0\\) immediately left. Thus \\(w\\geq0\\) throughout the required region.</p><p>Writing \\(z_1=m/n&gt;1\\),\n\\[\n1-\\frac2{z_1}+\\ell(2z_1-1)\n\\geq2z_1-\\frac2{z_1}&gt;0.\n\\]\nTogether with \\((m-n)\\mathscr S&gt;0\\), this gives \\(D_*&gt;0\\). Hence no zero occurs in this case.</p><p>**Case 3: \\(m&gt;n\\) and \\(0&lt;\\ell&lt;1\\).** Again \\(d&lt;0\\). It suffices to prove\n\\[\nw\\geq w_*\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad D_*&gt;0.\n\\]\nAt a zero this implies \\(w&lt;w_*\\), and Lemma 7.2 then gives the required sign.</p><p>Normalize \\(n=1\\), \\(m=z_1&gt;1\\). More precisely, fix the original \\(N=n&gt;0\\), and put \\(y=N\\widetilde y\\), \\(u=N^2\\widetilde u\\). Then\n\\[\n\\widetilde d=Nd,\\quad \\widetilde b=N^3b,\\quad\n\\widetilde m=m/N,\\quad \\widetilde n=n/N,\\quad\n\\widetilde\\ell=\\ell,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\widetilde w=Nw,\\quad\n\\widetilde{\\mathscr S}=N^2\\mathscr S,\\quad\n\\widetilde D_*=ND_*,\\quad \\widetilde w_*=Nw_*.\n\\]\nThus the equations and required signs are preserved.</p><p>In these units,\n\\[\nw_*=\\frac{2(\\ell z_1^3(z_1-2)+2z_1-1)}{z_1^2(z_1+1)}&gt;0.\n\\]\nFor \\(z_1\\geq2\\) this is immediate. For \\(1&lt;z_1&lt;2\\), replacing \\(\\ell&lt;1\\) by one decreases the numerator to \\((z_1-1)^3(z_1+1)&gt;0\\).</p><p>For fixed \\(\\mathscr S\\),\n\\[\nD_*=(z_1-1)\\mathscr S+\n[1-2/z_1+\\ell(2z_1-1)]w+\\frac{z_1+1}{2}w^2.\n\\]\nIts derivative in \\(w\\), evaluated at \\(w_*\\) and multiplied by \\(z_1^2\\), is\n\\[\nJ(\\ell,z_1)=2\\ell z_1^4-2\\ell z_1^3-\\ell z_1^2+z_1^2+2z_1-2.\n\\]\nThis is affine in \\(\\ell\\). For \\(\\xi=z_1-1&gt;0\\),\n\\[\nJ(0,1+\\xi)=\\xi^2+4\\xi+1,\\qquad\nJ(1,1+\\xi)=2\\xi(\\xi+2)(\\xi^2+\\xi+1).\n\\]\nBoth are positive. Hence \\(D_*\\) is strictly increasing for \\(w\\geq w_*\\).</p><p>Set\n\\[\nT=2\\ell z_1^3-\\ell z_1^2-z_1+2,\\qquad\nA=2\\ell z_1^3-3\\ell z_1^2+3z_1-2,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathscr S_0=\\frac32(\\ell^2-z_1^{-2}).\n\\]\nSubstitution gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;(z_1-1)\\mathscr S_0+\n[1-2/z_1+\\ell(2z_1-1)]w_*+\\frac{z_1+1}{2}w_*^2\\\\\n&amp;\\hspace{30mm}=\\frac{z_1-1}{2z_1^4}AT,\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\Delta_0:=\\frac1{z_1}-\\ell-w_*\n=-\\frac{z_1-1}{(z_1+1)z_1^2}T.\n\\]\nFor the first factorization, multiplication of its left side by \\(2z_1^4(z_1+1)\\) gives\n\\[\n(z_1^2-1)\\bigl[\n\\ell^2z_1^4(2z_1-3)(2z_1-1)\n+4\\ell z_1^2(z_1^2-1)\n+(3z_1-2)(2-z_1)\\bigr].\n\\]\nThe bracket is \\(AT\\), since its cross coefficient is\n\\[\nz_1^2[(2z_1-3)(2-z_1)+(3z_1-2)(2z_1-1)]\n=4z_1^2(z_1^2-1).\n\\]\nFor the second, the numerator is\n\\[\n(z_1-1)[z_1-2-\\ell z_1^2(2z_1-1)]=-(z_1-1)T.\n\\]\nAlso \\(A&gt;0\\), because it is affine in \\(\\ell\\) and\n\\[\nA(0,1+\\xi)=3\\xi+1,\\qquad\nA(1,1+\\xi)=\\xi(2\\xi^2+3\\xi+3).\n\\]</p><p>Assume \\(w\\geq w_*\\). If \\(T\\geq0\\) and \\(d^2\\geq4bz_1\\), Lemma 7.3 gives\n\\[\n\\Delta_0\\geq\\frac1{z_1}-\\ell-w\n\\geq-d\\ell+2\\nu&gt;2\\nu\\geq0,\n\\]\ncontradicting the formula for \\(\\Delta_0\\). Hence \\(d^2&lt;4bz_1\\), and\n\\[\n\\mathscr S-\\mathscr S_0\n=\\left(2bz_1-\\frac{d^2}{2}\\right)\n+\\ell^2\\left(2b+\\frac{d^2}{2}-d\\right)-\\frac d{z_1}&gt;0.\n\\]\nThe baseline factorization is nonnegative because \\(A&gt;0,T\\geq0\\). The strict improvement in \\(\\mathscr S\\), then monotonicity in \\(w\\), yields \\(D_*&gt;0\\).</p><p>If \\(T&lt;0\\), then\n\\[\nz_1-2&gt;\\ell z_1^2(2z_1-1)&gt;0.\n\\]\nThus \\(z_1&gt;2\\) and \\(z_1-2&gt;3\\ell\\). Since \\(X=\\kappa P+d\\) and \\(\\kappa P&gt;0\\),\n\\[\nd&lt;\\frac{\\ell/z_1-1}{1+\\ell},\\qquad\n-dz_1&gt;\\frac{z_1-\\ell}{1+\\ell}&gt;2.\n\\]\nRewrite the Schwarzian formula as\n\\[\n\\mathscr S=\n\\left(2bz_1-\\frac{d^2}{2}\\right)\n+\\ell^2\\left(2b+\\frac{d^2}{2}-d+\\frac32\\right)\n-\\frac d{z_1}-\\frac3{2z_1^2}.\n\\]\nIf the first parentheses are nonnegative, then \\(-d/z_1&gt;2/z_1^2\\) gives \\(\\mathscr S&gt;0\\). If they are negative, put \\(\\nu=\\sqrt{d^2/4-bz_1}&gt;0\\). The same endpoint estimate gives \\(2\\nu&lt;\\Delta_0\\), while \\(w_*&gt;0,\\ell&gt;0\\) give \\(\\Delta_0&lt;1/z_1\\). Hence\n\\[\n2bz_1-\\frac{d^2}{2}=-2\\nu^2&gt;-\\frac1{2z_1^2}.\n\\]\nTogether with \\(-d/z_1&gt;2/z_1^2\\), this strictly dominates \\(-3/(2z_1^2)\\); the remaining term is positive, so \\(\\mathscr S&gt;0\\). Since \\(z_1&gt;2\\) and \\(w\\geq w_*&gt;0\\), every term of \\(D_*\\) is positive.</p><p>The three cases exhaust the endpoint configurations. At a zero, Cases 1 and 3 give\n\\[\n\\frac{w_*-w}{m-n}&gt;0;\n\\]\nthe diagonal and Case 2 contain no zeros. Lemma 7.2 proves the assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "Lemma 5.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "At every positive base height, locally uniformly as \\(r\\downarrow0\\), \\( \\kappa(h,r)=\\phi''(h)+\\frac27\\phi'''(h)r^2+O(r^3), \\) \\( \\kappa_r(h,r)=\\frac47\\phi'''(h)r+O(r^2), \\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\phi\\in C^\\infty((0,\\infty))\\), \\(k=\\phi'\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\lambda(h,r),\\kappa(h,r)\\) be the unique quadratic fit of Proposition 6.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>At every positive base height, locally uniformly as \\(r\\downarrow0\\),\n\\[\n\\kappa(h,r)=\\phi''(h)+\\frac27\\phi'''(h)r^2+O(r^3),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\kappa_r(h,r)=\\frac47\\phi'''(h)r+O(r^2),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\lambda(h,r)=k(h)-\\frac1{35}\\phi'''(h)r^4+O(r^5).\n\\]\nMore precisely, near each \\((h_0,0)\\), \\(h_0&gt;0\\), there are smooth \\(A,B\\) such that\n\\[\n\\lambda(h,r)=k(h)-\\frac1{35}\\phi'''(h)r^4+r^5A(h,r),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\kappa(h,r)=\\phi''(h)+\\frac27\\phi'''(h)r^2+r^3B(h,r).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix \\(h_0&gt;0\\), and use the signed smooth width-one profile family\n\\[\ng(h,\\rho,s)=\\rho s\\int_0^1\\phi'(h+\\tau\\rho^2s)\\,d\\tau\n\\]\nfrom Proposition 2. Its scaled arc data, and those of the finite-parameter perturbations below, are smooth near the same transverse parabolic arc.</p><p>For a scaled profile \\(\\psi\\), let \\(\\mathcal A(\\psi)\\) be its midpoint and midpoint-width derivative at base zero and width one. For\n\\[\n\\psi(s)=a_1s+a_2s^2/2,\n\\]\nthe derivative of this pair at \\((a_1,a_2)=(0,0)\\) is\n\\[\nJ=\\begin{pmatrix}1/3&amp;1/15\\\\2/3&amp;4/15\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nIt is nonsingular. The cubic profile variation gives\n\\[\nb=\\begin{pmatrix}1/105\\\\6/105\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad\nJ^{-1}b=\\begin{pmatrix}-1/35\\\\2/7\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]</p><p>Taylor expansion with a smooth remainder gives\n\\[\ng(h,\\rho,s)\n=\\rho k(h)s+\\rho^3\\phi''(h)\\frac{s^2}{2}\n+\\rho^5\\phi'''(h)\\frac{s^3}{6}+\\rho^7R(h,\\rho,s).\n\\]\nWrite\n\\[\ng_2=\\rho k(h)s+\\rho^3\\phi''(h)s^2/2,\\qquad\n\\eta=\\phi'''(h)s^3/6+\\rho^2R.\n\\]\nThen \\(g=g_2+\\rho^5\\eta\\). Apply the local inverse defined by \\(J\\) to the data of \\(g_2+\\varepsilon\\eta\\), and denote the fitted scaled coefficients by \\(a_1(h,\\rho,\\varepsilon)\\), \\(a_2(h,\\rho,\\varepsilon)\\). At \\(\\varepsilon=0\\), they are exactly \\(\\rho k(h)\\), \\(\\rho^3\\phi''(h)\\). Taylor’s integral formula gives\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix}\na_1(h,\\rho,\\rho^5)-\\rho k(h)\\\\\na_2(h,\\rho,\\rho^5)-\\rho^3\\phi''(h)\n\\end{pmatrix}\n=\\rho^5q(h,\\rho),\n\\]\nwhere \\(q\\) is smooth and\n\\[\nq(h,0)=\\phi'''(h)J^{-1}b\n=\\phi'''(h)\\begin{pmatrix}-1/35\\\\2/7\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nA further smooth Taylor factorization writes\n\\[\nq(h,\\rho)\n=\\phi'''(h)\\begin{pmatrix}-1/35\\\\2/7\\end{pmatrix}\n+\\rho\\begin{pmatrix}A(h,\\rho)\\\\B(h,\\rho)\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]</p><p>For \\(\\rho=r&gt;0\\), the scaled actual data are \\((M(h,r)/r,v(h,r))\\). Scaling the quadratic fit gives coefficients \\((r\\lambda,r^3\\kappa)\\). Uniqueness in Proposition 5 identifies them with the local coefficients. Dividing the corrections by \\(r\\) and \\(r^3\\) proves the smooth formulas. Finally,\n\\[\n\\kappa_r\n=\\frac47\\phi'''(h)r+3r^2B+r^3B_r,\n\\]\nwhich proves the differentiated estimate.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "Proposition 5.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "If \\(\\phi'''(u)>0\\) for every \\(u>0\\), then, for all \\(h,r>0\\), \\( \\lambda(h,r)<k(h),\\qquad \\kappa_r(h,r)>0. \\) If \\(\\phi'''(u)<0\\) everywhere, both inequalities reverse.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\phi\\in C^\\infty((0,\\infty))\\), \\(k=\\phi'\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\lambda(h,r),\\kappa(h,r)\\) be its quadratic fit from Proposition 6.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If \\(\\phi'''(u)&gt;0\\) for every \\(u&gt;0\\), then, for all \\(h,r&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\lambda(h,r)&lt;k(h),\\qquad \\kappa_r(h,r)&gt;0.\n\\]\nIf \\(\\phi'''(u)&lt;0\\) everywhere, both inequalities reverse.</p>", "proof": "<p>Assume \\(\\phi'''&gt;0\\). Fix an arc with base \\(h\\), width \\(r\\), and peak \\(t\\), and parametrize its characteristic by outward width \\(s\\in(0,r]\\). Write\n\\[\n\\theta(s)=\\operatorname{arctanh}\\frac{M(h(s),s)}s,\\qquad\nL(s)=\\operatorname{arctanh}v(h(s),s),\\qquad\nc(s)=\\phi''(h(s)).\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nc'(s)=-s\\operatorname{sech}^2\\theta(s)\\,\\phi'''(h(s))&lt;0.\n\\]\nThus \\(c\\) extends continuously to zero with \\(c(0)=\\phi''(t)\\), and is strictly decreasing.</p><p>Hold the reference curvature equal to the final fitted value \\(\\kappa=\\kappa(h,r)\\), and put\n\\[\nE=k(h(s))-J_\\kappa(s,\\theta(s)),\\qquad\na=(J_\\kappa)_\\theta(s,\\theta(s))&gt;0,\n\\]\n\\[\nf=-s\\operatorname{sech}^2\\theta(s)(c(s)-\\kappa).\n\\]\nProposition 4 gives\n\\[\nE'+aE=f,\\qquad E(s)\\to0,\n\\]\nwith its convergent positive-kernel integral formula.</p><p>If \\(\\kappa\\geq c(0)\\), then \\(f&gt;0\\) and \\(E&gt;0\\). If \\(\\kappa\\leq c(r)\\), then \\(f&lt;0\\) before \\(r\\), and \\(E&lt;0\\) through \\(r\\). Otherwise there is a unique \\(s_0\\in(0,r)\\) where \\(c(s_0)=\\kappa\\). Here \\(f&lt;0\\) before \\(s_0\\), \\(f&gt;0\\) after it, and \\(E&lt;0\\) through \\(s_0\\). Moreover,\n\\[\n\\frac d{ds}\\left[E(s)\\exp\\left(\\int_{s_0}^sa(q)\\,dq\\right)\\right]\n=f(s)\\exp\\left(\\int_{s_0}^sa(q)\\,dq\\right)&gt;0\n\\]\nfor \\(s&gt;s_0\\). Therefore \\(E\\) either remains negative through the final width, possibly vanishing there, or crosses once before \\(r\\) and is positive thereafter. It cannot vanish on an interval, since its equation would force \\(c=\\kappa\\) there.</p><p>Define \\(z(s)\\) by \\(\\theta(s)=\\Theta_\\kappa(s,z(s))\\). Then\n\\[\nz'=\\frac{E}{\\partial_z\\Theta_\\kappa},\\qquad\n\\partial_z\\Theta_\\kappa&gt;0.\n\\]\nThe constant label \\(z_*=z(r)\\) describes the reference with the same final angle, and its final slope is \\(\\lambda(h,r)\\). The fit also matches \\(v\\), hence \\(L\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\int_0^r\\frac{\\sinh(2\\theta(s))}{s}\\,ds\n=\n\\int_0^r\\frac{\\sinh(2\\Theta_\\kappa(s,z_*))}{s}\\,ds.\n\\]\nBoth integrals converge because the angles are \\(O(s)\\) near their smooth peaks.</p><p>If \\(z(s)\\) were monotone and nonconstant, reference monotonicity would order the two integrands in one direction, strictly on an open subinterval, contradicting equality. The first two forcing cases are impossible. In the two-sign case, \\(E\\) must cross before \\(r\\); otherwise \\(z\\) is strictly decreasing on \\((0,r)\\), giving the same contradiction. A constant label would force constant \\(c\\), also impossible. Hence\n\\[\nE(r)=k(h)-\\lambda(h,r)&gt;0.\n\\]</p><p>Set, as a function of independent model parameters,\n\\[\nC(\\lambda,\\kappa,r)=\\frac{R}{G}.\n\\]\nProposition 7 gives the explicit partial derivative \\(C_r\\leq0\\). Differentiate\n\\[\nD\\kappa=-C(\\lambda-k(h))\n\\]\nat fixed \\(h\\), accounting for\n\\[\n\\partial_r(D\\kappa)=D(\\kappa_r)-\\alpha_r\\kappa_r.\n\\]\nUsing \\(\\lambda_r=-Q\\kappa_r/P\\), one obtains\n\\[\nD(\\kappa_r)=T\\kappa_r-C_r(\\lambda-k(h)),\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nT=\\alpha_r+\\frac{CQ}{P}\n-\\left(C_\\kappa-\\frac QP C_\\lambda\\right)(\\lambda-k(h)).\n\\]\nHere \\(\\alpha_r\\) is the derivative of the actual \\(\\alpha(h,r)\\); the derivatives of \\(C\\) are model partial derivatives evaluated at the fit. All coefficients are smooth at positive height and width.</p><p>On the characteristic, put \\(w(s)=\\kappa_r(h(s),s)\\). Proposition 8 gives\n\\[\nw(s)=\\frac47\\phi'''(h(s))s+O(s^2)&gt;0\n\\]\nat small positive \\(s\\). The transport equation becomes\n\\[\nw'=-\\frac T\\alpha w+\\frac{C_r}{\\alpha}(\\lambda-k),\n\\qquad\n\\frac{C_r}{\\alpha}(\\lambda-k)\\geq0.\n\\]\nStarting from a small \\(s_1&gt;0\\) with \\(w(s_1)&gt;0\\), multiply by\n\\[\n\\mu(s)=\\exp\\left(\\int_{s_1}^s\\frac T\\alpha\\,dq\\right).\n\\]\nThen \\((\\mu w)'\\geq0\\), so \\(w(r)&gt;0\\).</p><p>Finally, reflection \\(y\\mapsto-y\\) changes \\(\\phi\\) to \\(-\\phi\\), and uniqueness of fitting changes \\((\\lambda,\\kappa)\\) to \\((-\\lambda,-\\kappa)\\). Applying the proved case to \\(-\\phi\\) gives the reversed inequalities when \\(\\phi'''&lt;0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "Proposition 5.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "If \\( \\frac d{du}\\left(\\frac{k(u)-d}{u}\\right)>0 \\qquad(u>0), \\) then \\( \\lambda(h,r)-h\\kappa(h,r)<d \\qquad(h,r>0). \\) If the displayed derivative is strictly negative everywhere, then …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\phi\\in C^\\infty((0,\\infty))\\), \\(k=\\phi'\\), and let \\(\\lambda,\\kappa\\) be its quadratic fit.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(d\\in\\mathbb R\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If\n\\[\n\\frac d{du}\\left(\\frac{k(u)-d}{u}\\right)&gt;0\n\\qquad(u&gt;0),\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\n\\lambda(h,r)-h\\kappa(h,r)&lt;d\n\\qquad(h,r&gt;0).\n\\]\nIf the displayed derivative is strictly negative everywhere, then\n\\[\n\\lambda(h,r)-h\\kappa(h,r)&gt;d\n\\qquad(h,r&gt;0).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Assume the derivative is positive, and set\n\\[\nI(h,r)=\\lambda(h,r)-h\\kappa(h,r)-d.\n\\]\nFix a characteristic with peak \\(t\\). Proposition 8 gives\n\\[\n\\lim_{s\\downarrow0}I(h(s),s)\n=k(t)-t\\phi''(t)-d\n=-t^2\\left.\\frac d{du}\\left(\\frac{k(u)-d}{u}\\right)\\right|_{u=t}&lt;0.\n\\]\nThus \\(I&lt;0\\) near the peak.</p><p>At any positive-height zero of \\(I\\), one has \\(\\lambda=d+h\\kappa\\). We claim\n\\[\n\\kappa&gt;\\frac{k(h)-d}{h}.\n\\]\nIf the reverse weak inequality held, then for every \\(u&gt;h\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{k(u)-d}{u}&gt;\\frac{k(h)-d}{h}\\geq\\kappa,\n\\]\nso\n\\[\nk(u)&gt;d+u\\kappa=\\lambda+(u-h)\\kappa.\n\\]\nIntegration gives\n\\[\n\\phi(u)-\\phi(h)&gt;\n\\lambda(u-h)+\\frac\\kappa2(u-h)^2.\n\\]\nThe strict translated-profile comparison of Proposition 2 makes the actual midpoint strictly larger than the fitted quadratic midpoint, a contradiction. Thus the claim holds, and \\(\\lambda&gt;k(h)\\) at any zero.</p><p>Proposition 6 gives\n\\[\nDI=\\frac{S+hR}{G}(\\lambda-k(h)).\n\\]\nAt a zero, this is strictly positive, so the outward derivative \\(-DI/\\alpha\\) is strictly negative. A first zero after the initially negative interval would have nonnegative outward derivative. This contradiction proves \\(I&lt;0\\) everywhere.</p><p>For a strictly negative derivative, apply the proved assertion to \\(-\\phi\\) and \\(-d\\), using reflection of the fitted parameters.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "Lemma 5.4", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\min_{h\\leq u\\leq t}\\phi''(u) \\leq\\kappa(h,r)\\leq \\max_{h\\leq u\\leq t}\\phi''(u). \\) If \\(\\phi''\\) is nonconstant on \\([h,t]\\), both inequalities are strict.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\phi\\in C^\\infty((0,\\infty))\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let an arc have base height \\(h&gt;0\\), peak height \\(t&gt;h\\), width \\(r\\), and fitted curvature \\(\\kappa(h,r)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\min_{h\\leq u\\leq t}\\phi''(u)\n\\leq\\kappa(h,r)\\leq\n\\max_{h\\leq u\\leq t}\\phi''(u).\n\\]\nIf \\(\\phi''\\) is nonconstant on \\([h,t]\\), both inequalities are strict.</p>", "proof": "<p>Write\n\\[\nm=\\min_{[h,t]}\\phi'',\\qquad b=\\max_{[h,t]}\\phi''.\n\\]\nIf the fitted curvature were less than \\(m\\), actual curvature would be strictly larger than this reference curvature along the whole characteristic. Proposition 4 would make the actual final \\(L\\) strictly larger than the reference \\(L\\) with the same final angle, contradicting the fit. Curvature greater than \\(b\\) gives the reversed contradiction.</p><p>If \\(\\phi''\\) is nonconstant and \\(\\kappa=m\\), the forcing in the comparison equation of Proposition 4 is nonpositive and strictly negative on an interior width interval. Its integral formula gives \\(E\\leq0\\), with strict negativity on a nonempty interval. The reference label is nonincreasing and nonconstant. Relative to its final constant label, actual earlier angles are no smaller, and are strictly larger on an open interval. Their \\(L\\)-integral is therefore strictly larger, again contradicting the fit. The case \\(\\kappa=b\\) reverses the inequalities.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "the upper bound proved in Section 6", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The system \\( \\dot x=y-F(x),\\qquad \\dot y=-x \\) has at most two limit cycles.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(F\\in\\mathbb R[x]\\), \\(\\deg F\\leq5\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Limit cycles are geometrically distinct isolated periodic orbits, as in Theorem 1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The system\n\\[\n\\dot x=y-F(x),\\qquad \\dot y=-x\n\\]\nhas at most two limit cycles.</p>", "proof": "<p>Translate \\(y\\) by \\(F(0)\\) so that \\(F(0)=0\\). If\n\\[\nX(t)=-x(-t),\\qquad Y(t)=y(-t),\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\n\\dot X=Y-F(-X),\\qquad \\dot Y=-X.\n\\]\nReflection and time reversal preserve periodic-orbit geometry and isolation, while negating the coefficient of \\(x^5\\). Assume that coefficient is nonnegative.</p><p>Write\n\\[\nF(x)=\\sum_{j=1}^5f_jx^j.\n\\]\nFor \\(u=x^2/2\\), the two profiles are\n\\[\n\\phi_\\pm(u)=d_0u+b_0u^2\\pm p(u),\n\\qquad\np(u)=eu^{1/2}+cu^{3/2}+au^{5/2},\\qquad a\\geq0,\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nd_0=2f_2,\\quad b_0=4f_4,\\quad\ne=\\sqrt2\\,f_1,\\quad c=2^{3/2}f_3,\\quad a=2^{5/2}f_5.\n\\]\nThey are smooth for \\(u&gt;0\\), continuous at zero, and vanish there.</p><p>Let \\(J=I_+\\cap I_-\\) be the common transverse boundary-width interval of Proposition 3. If it is empty, there are no periodic orbits. On \\(J\\), define the boundary fitted parameters by the inverse in Proposition 5 applied to\n\\[\n(M_\\pm(0,r),M_{\\pm,r}(0,r),r).\n\\]\nThese data are interior to the fit domain. Boundary convergence and continuity of the inverse identify the parameters with positive-height limits. Set\n\\[\n\\Delta(r)=M_+(0,r)-M_-(0,r).\n\\]\nBy Proposition 3, it suffices to bound its isolated zeros.</p><p>For the conditional model derivative from Proposition 5, implicit differentiation gives\n\\[\n\\widehat v_M=\\frac RP&gt;0,\\qquad\n\\widehat v_\\kappa=\\frac GP&gt;0.\n\\]\nFor each actual profile,\n\\[\n\\frac d{dr}M_\\pm(0,r)\n=\\widehat v(r,M_\\pm(0,r),\\kappa_\\pm(0,r)).\n\\]\nPropositions 3 and 5 justify the smooth boundary identities.</p><p>**Case 1: \\(e\\geq0\\), \\(c\\geq0\\).** Either \\(p\\equiv0\\), so both midpoint functions coincide and no zero is isolated, or \\(p(u)&gt;0\\) for all \\(u&gt;0\\). In the latter situation, suppose two positive arches have the same endpoints \\(A&lt;B\\) at height zero. Their difference \\(w=u_+-u_-\\) satisfies\n\\[\nw'=[d_0+b_0(u_++u_-)]w+p(u_+)+p(u_-),\n\\qquad w(A)=w(B)=0.\n\\]\nThe coefficient is continuous on \\([A,B]\\), and the inhomogeneous term is strictly positive on \\((A,B)\\). The integrating-factor formula from \\(A\\) gives \\(w(B)&gt;0\\), a contradiction. Thus no periodic orbit exists.</p><p>**Case 2: \\(e\\geq0\\), \\(c&lt;0\\).** Here\n\\[\np'''(u)=\\frac{3e-3cu+15au^2}{8u^{5/2}}&gt;0.\n\\]\nProposition 9 gives\n\\[\n\\partial_r\\kappa_+(h,r)&gt;0,\\qquad\n\\partial_r\\kappa_-(h,r)&lt;0.\n\\]\nPass to height-zero limits at any two specified widths in \\(J\\). Then\n\\[\nq(r)=\\kappa_+(0,r)-\\kappa_-(0,r)\n\\]\nis nondecreasing.</p><p>Subtracting the two midpoint derivative identities and applying the fundamental theorem of calculus first in \\(M\\), then in \\(\\kappa\\), gives\n\\[\n\\Delta'=A(r)\\Delta+B(r)q,\\qquad B(r)&gt;0,\n\\]\nwhere, suppressing \\((0,r)\\) on actual data,\n\\[\nA(r)=\\int_0^1\n\\widehat v_M(r,M_-+\\tau(M_+-M_-),\\kappa_+)\\,d\\tau,\n\\]\n\\[\nB(r)=\\int_0^1\n\\widehat v_\\kappa(r,M_-,\\kappa_-+\\tau(\\kappa_+-\\kappa_-))\\,d\\tau.\n\\]\nThe midpoint segment stays in \\((-r,r)\\), and curvature is unrestricted, so these coefficients are continuous and \\(B&gt;0\\).</p><p>Fix \\(r_*\\in J\\), and multiply \\(\\Delta\\) by\n\\[\n\\exp\\left(-\\int_{r_*}^rA(s)\\,ds\\right).\n\\]\nIts derivative is a continuous positive factor times the nondecreasing function \\(q\\). Lemma 12.1 gives at most two isolated zeros, including possible multiple roots and zero intervals.</p><p>**Case 3: \\(e&lt;0\\), \\(c\\geq0\\).** Now\n\\[\np''(u)=\\frac{-e+3cu+15au^2}{4u^{3/2}}&gt;0,\\qquad\np''(u)\\to+\\infty\\quad(u\\downarrow0).\n\\]\nFix \\(r_0\\in J\\). The positive-height peaks at width \\(r_0\\) approach finite positive peaks as \\(h\\downarrow0\\). Choose \\(T\\) larger than both limiting peaks and all sufficiently nearby peaks. On \\((0,T]\\), \\(p''\\) has a positive lower bound \\(\\delta\\). Along the relevant arcs,\n\\[\n\\phi_+''\\geq2b_0+\\delta,\\qquad\n\\phi_-''\\leq2b_0-\\delta.\n\\]\nProposition 11 and passage to the limit give\n\\[\n\\kappa_+(0,r_0)\\geq2b_0+\\delta\n&gt;2b_0-\\delta\\geq\\kappa_-(0,r_0).\n\\]\nAt every zero of \\(\\Delta\\), equal midpoints and \\(\\widehat v_\\kappa&gt;0\\) imply \\(\\Delta'&gt;0\\). Lemma 12.1 gives at most one zero.</p><p>**Case 4: \\(e&lt;0\\), \\(c&lt;0\\).** One has\n\\[\nup''(u)-p'(u)\n=\\frac{-3e-3cu+5au^2}{4\\sqrt u}&gt;0.\n\\]\nFor \\(k_\\pm=\\phi_\\pm'\\), this is\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{k_+(h)-d_0}{h}\\right)'&gt;0,\\qquad\n\\left(\\frac{k_-(h)-d_0}{h}\\right)'&lt;0.\n\\]\nProposition 10 yields\n\\[\n\\lambda_+(h,r)-h\\kappa_+(h,r)&lt;d_0,\\qquad\n\\lambda_-(h,r)-h\\kappa_-(h,r)&gt;d_0.\n\\]</p><p>To retain strictness at height zero, fix a transverse plus-profile peak and let \\(r(h)\\to r_0\\in J\\) be its width. Boundary data converge to an interior triple, so Proposition 5 gives finite limits of \\(\\lambda,\\kappa\\). Put\n\\[\nI(h)=\\lambda(h,r(h))-h\\kappa(h,r(h))-d_0.\n\\]\nProposition 6 gives, along this fixed-peak characteristic,\n\\[\n\\frac{dI}{dh}=\\frac{S+hR}{G}(\\lambda-k_+(h)).\n\\]\nSince \\(e&lt;0\\), \\(k_+(h)\\to-\\infty\\), while \\(\\lambda\\) stays bounded. Thus \\(dI/dh&gt;0\\) for all sufficiently small positive \\(h\\). Choose such an \\(h_1\\). Then\n\\[\nI(h)\\leq I(h_1)&lt;0\\qquad(0&lt;h&lt;h_1).\n\\]\nTaking the limit gives \\(\\lambda_+(0,r_0)&lt;d_0\\). On the minus side, \\(k_-(h)\\to+\\infty\\); the reversed argument gives \\(\\lambda_-(0,r_0)&gt;d_0\\). Hence throughout \\(J\\),\n\\[\n\\lambda_+(0,r)&lt;d_0&lt;\\lambda_-(0,r).\n\\]</p><p>At a zero of \\(\\Delta\\), model midpoints agree. Since \\(H_\\lambda,H_\\kappa&gt;0\\), the slope separation forces\n\\[\n\\kappa_+(0,r)&gt;\\kappa_-(0,r):\n\\]\notherwise increasing \\(\\lambda_+\\) to \\(\\lambda_-\\), then \\(\\kappa_+\\) to \\(\\kappa_-\\), would strictly increase \\(H\\). Thus \\(\\Delta'&gt;0\\) at every zero, and Lemma 12.1 again gives at most one.</p><p>The four cases exhaust all \\(e,c\\in\\mathbb R\\), with \\(a\\geq0\\), including zero leading coefficients. Proposition 3 therefore proves the two-cycle upper bound without any amplitude or multiplicity restriction.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.1", "orig": "Lemma 6.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Let \\(f\\) be continuously differentiable on an open interval.", "statement": "<p>Let \\(f\\) be continuously differentiable on an open interval.</p><p class=\"li num\">1. If\n   \\[\n   f'=bq,\n   \\]\n   where \\(b\\) is continuous and positive and \\(q\\) is continuous and nondecreasing, then \\(f\\) has at most two isolated zeros.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. If \\(f'\\) is strictly positive at every zero of \\(f\\), then \\(f\\) has at most one zero.</p>", "proof": "<p>For the first assertion, the sets where \\(q\\) is negative, zero, and positive occur in that order. Its zero set is an interval, possibly empty or a singleton. Accordingly, \\(f\\) strictly decreases, is constant, and strictly increases on the respective sets. Each strict part has at most one zero.</p><p>If the constant part has nonzero value, it contributes none. If it has positive length and value zero, all its points and its endpoints within the domain are nonisolated zeros, and neither strict part contributes another zero. If it is a singleton where \\(f=0\\), that point is the sole minimum zero. Thus there are at most two isolated zeros.</p><p>For the second assertion, suppose \\(r_1&lt;r_2\\) are zeros. Positive derivatives give \\(r_1&lt;a&lt;b&lt;r_2\\) with \\(f(a)&gt;0\\), \\(f(b)&lt;0\\). The first zero \\(c\\) in \\([a,b]\\) exists. Since \\(f&gt;0\\) on \\([a,c)\\), its derivative at \\(c\\) is nonpositive, a contradiction.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 13", "orig": "the sharpness construction of Section 7", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every sufficiently small positive \\(\\varepsilon\\), the system has two geometrically distinct hyperbolic limit cycles.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), set\n  \\[\n  F_\\varepsilon(x)=\\varepsilon f(x),\\qquad\n  f(x)=4x-\\frac{20}{3}x^3+\\frac85x^5.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Consider\n  \\[\n  \\dot x=y-F_\\varepsilon(x),\\qquad \\dot y=-x.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every sufficiently small positive \\(\\varepsilon\\), the system has two geometrically distinct hyperbolic limit cycles. Their positive \\(y\\)-axis intercepts approach \\(1\\) and \\(2\\), respectively, as \\(\\varepsilon\\to0\\). The inner cycle repels and the outer cycle attracts.</p>", "proof": "<p>At \\(\\varepsilon=0\\), the solution through \\((0,s)\\), \\(s&gt;0\\), is\n\\[\nx(t)=s\\sin t,\\qquad y(t)=s\\cos t.\n\\]\nFor \\(s\\) near either one or two, smooth flow dependence near the compact circle and transversality at \\(2\\pi\\) give a smooth return time \\(\\tau(s,\\varepsilon)\\) near \\(2\\pi\\) and a smooth positive return ordinate \\(P(s,\\varepsilon)\\). These are the first positive-axis returns for small \\(|\\varepsilon|\\): outside small time neighborhoods of the axis crossings, unperturbed \\(x\\) is bounded away from zero, while transversality gives a unique nearby crossing in each neighborhood. After shrinking, the two \\(s\\)-neighborhoods have one common parameter range.</p><p>For\n\\[\nE=\\frac{x^2+y^2}{2},\n\\]\nthe exact energy identity is\n\\[\n\\dot E=-\\varepsilon xf(x).\n\\]\nConsequently\n\\[\n\\frac{P(s,\\varepsilon)^2-s^2}{2}\n=\\varepsilon Q(s,\\varepsilon),\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nQ(s,\\varepsilon)\n=-\\int_0^{\\tau(s,\\varepsilon)}\nx(t;s,\\varepsilon)f(x(t;s,\\varepsilon))\\,dt.\n\\]\nThis smooth integral defines the quotient at \\(\\varepsilon=0\\) exactly. Using\n\\[\n\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\sin^2t\\,dt=\\pi,\\qquad\n\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\sin^4t\\,dt=\\frac{3\\pi}{4},\\qquad\n\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\sin^6t\\,dt=\\frac{5\\pi}{8},\n\\]\none obtains\n\\[\nQ(s,0)=-\\pi s^2(4-5s^2+s^4).\n\\]\nIts roots at one and two are simple:\n\\[\nQ_s(1,0)=6\\pi,\\qquad Q_s(2,0)=-48\\pi.\n\\]\nThe implicit function theorem gives roots \\(s_1(\\varepsilon)\\), \\(s_2(\\varepsilon)\\) in disjoint positive neighborhoods, for the same sufficiently small \\(|\\varepsilon|\\).</p><p>Fix a nonzero positive \\(\\varepsilon\\) in that range. At these roots, \\(P+s&gt;0\\), so the energy identity implies \\(P=s\\). Each gives a periodic orbit. Differentiation at a root gives\n\\[\ns(P_s-1)=\\varepsilon Q_s\\neq0.\n\\]\nThus each fixed point is simple and isolated. The orbits are geometrically distinct because their positive intercepts lie in disjoint neighborhoods, and Proposition 3 gives only one positive intercept per periodic orbit.</p><p>After decreasing the upper bound on \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), continuity from \\(P_s(s,0)=1\\) gives \\(P_s&gt;0\\) at both fixed points. The signs of \\(Q_s\\) give \\(P_s&gt;1\\) on the inner cycle and \\(0&lt;P_s&lt;1\\) on the outer cycle. Thus both cycles are hyperbolic, the inner repelling and the outer attracting.</p>"}], "edges": [[1, 4, "cite"], [1, 7, "cite"], [1, 8, "cite"], [1, 10, "cite"], [1, 11, "cite"], [1, 14, "cite"], [1, 15, "cite"], [1, 16, "cite"], [2, 1, "cite"], [3, 1, "cite"], [4, 9, "cite"], [4, 10, "cite"], [4, 12, "cite"], [4, 17, "cite"], [4, 21, "cite"], [4, 23, "cite"], [5, 4, "cite"], [6, 4, "cite"], [7, 25, "cite"], [7, 27, "cite"], [8, 7, "cite"], [9, 12, "cite"], [9, 13, "cite"], [9, 16, "cite"], [9, 20, "cite"], [9, 22, "cite"], [9, 24, "cite"], [10, 9, "cite"], [11, 15, "cite"], [11, 16, "cite"], [11, 18, "cite"], [11, 21, "cite"], [11, 25, "cite"], [12, 11, "cite"], [13, 11, "cite"], [14, 11, "cite"], [15, 21, "cite"], [15, 22, "cite"], [15, 23, "cite"], [15, 25, "cite"], [16, 22, "cite"], [17, 16, "cite"], [18, 19, "cite"], [18, 20, "cite"], [19, 20, "cite"], [20, 16, "cite"], [21, 22, "cite"], [21, 23, "cite"], [22, 25, "cite"], [23, 25, "cite"], [24, 25, "cite"], [25, 0, "cite"], [26, 25, "cite"], [27, 0, "cite"]]});
